00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.982
So let me tell you a little about Emily.

00:03:25.982 --> 00:03:30.240
Emily Hanford is a senior correspondent

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.330
and producer for American Public Media.

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:36.483
Her work has appeared on "NPR"
and in the "New York Times",

00:03:36.483 --> 00:03:38.280
the "Washington Monthly",

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.280
the "Los Angeles Times"
and other publications.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.290
She has won numerous honors,

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:46.770
including a duPont-Columbia
University Award, and the,

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:50.640
Excellence in Media Reporting
on Education Research Award

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:54.060
from the American Educational
Research Association.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:55.410
Emily is a member of the

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:59.430
Education Writers Association
Journalist Advisory Board

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:04.430
and was a longtime mentor for
EWA's New to the Beat Program.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:05.970
For the past several years,

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:09.270
Emily has been reporting on
early reading instruction.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:12.517
Her 2018 podcast episode,

00:04:12.517 --> 00:04:15.510
"Hard Words, Why Aren't
Kids Being Taught To Read",

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:18.993
won the Inaugural Public
Service Award from the EWA.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:22.800
You can find all of her
reporting on reading

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.330
at apmreports.org\reading,

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:30.750
including her new podcast which I think

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:35.412
is a major hit and it's
compelling listening;

00:04:35.412 --> 00:04:40.412
"Sold a Story: How Teaching
Kids to Read Went So Wrong".

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:43.770
And I should just finally add
that I think it's very fair

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:48.770
to say that Emily has the
distinction of being a person

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:53.670
who has really changed the
national debate on a matter

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:56.460
of importance to everyone.

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:57.813
And that is,

00:04:59.967 --> 00:05:03.960
everyone should be so lucky as to be able

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.503
to see their work have
that kind of impact.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:11.220
So it's a wonderful privilege

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:14.610
to have Emily visiting with us today.

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:16.224
Welcome, Emily.

00:05:16.224 --> 00:05:18.930
- Thank you, thank you for having me.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:20.497
I've already gotten one text that says,

00:05:20.497 --> 00:05:22.020
"You're not in your usual office."

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:22.853
And it's true,

00:05:22.853 --> 00:05:23.820
I'm in New Haven.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:25.263
I'm here in person.

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:27.870
We're all on Zoom,

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:30.336
but I'm here in New Haven and
I'm really glad to be here.

00:05:30.336 --> 00:05:32.010
I've had a wonderful day so far.

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:34.290
I met a whole bunch of
students and faculty

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:38.100
and I'm really grateful
for the invitation.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.429
So, I'm just gonna talk
for a little while.

00:05:40.429 --> 00:05:43.150
I thought I would just tell
you a little bit about myself,

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:46.830
tell you sort of some of my story

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:48.660
about how I got interested in this topic

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:50.820
and some of what I have
learned about reading

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.250
and how it works and
how kids learn to do it.

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:54.780
'Cause actually, it's really fascinating

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:57.300
and I think many of you maybe don't know.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:58.620
I certainly didn't know.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:05:59.820
So you can put up the first slide.

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:01.110
I have some slides.

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:02.490
This is actually just,

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:04.890
the first one is just a couple of kids

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:06.270
in a classroom in California,

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:07.590
and this is the goal,

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:09.210
happy little readers, right.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.520
And the question has always been,

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:12.960
how does a little kid get there?

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:16.170
How does a kid learn to read?

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:18.630
So you can go to the next slide.

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:19.830
This is my kid.

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:20.663
I have two kids.

00:06:20.663 --> 00:06:21.496
I have two boys.

00:06:21.496 --> 00:06:23.070
So this is my little one.

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:26.010
And when my kids went off to school,

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:27.150
I really didn't think about

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:28.500
how they were gonna be taught to read.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:30.600
I definitely didn't think about

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:32.070
whether they were gonna be taught to read.

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:33.630
I just assumed they would,

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:35.400
and they did without much trouble.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:36.503
So this is my younger one.

00:06:36.503 --> 00:06:39.480
This is a few months after
he started kindergarten

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:41.910
and he's reading a
chapter book and I truly,

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:44.130
I don't have any idea how
he learned to do that.

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:45.600
It just kind of seemed to happen.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:47.130
It was like magic.

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:49.590
And school was actually
a really good experience

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:50.423
for this kid.

00:06:50.423 --> 00:06:51.660
He's now 20.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:53.130
He's a sophomore in college.

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:54.630
And it was for my older son, too,

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:55.650
who's a college graduate.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:57.079
It was for me, too,

00:06:57.079 --> 00:07:00.510
and I think the fact that
school was pretty good

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:02.700
for us had something to do with the fact

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:04.923
that reading came pretty easily.

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:10.110
But here's the thing that I
have learned that I didn't know.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:12.780
Reading doesn't come
easily to a lot of people

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:14.850
and it doesn't have to
do with intelligence.

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:16.500
There are lots of really smart people

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:17.850
who struggle to learn how to read.

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:19.410
And they can learn to read,

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:21.540
they can, but they really need someone

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:22.890
to teach them how to do it,

00:07:22.890 --> 00:07:24.630
to show them how it works.

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:26.820
And there was this moment when I started

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:29.550
to understand this in a way
that I never had before.

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:31.500
And it was actually here in Connecticut.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:33.960
It was the winter of 2016

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:35.820
and I was interviewing college students

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:38.850
who had been placed in
remedial reading classes.

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:40.860
And these were students who
graduated from high school.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:42.540
They went to college and they were told,

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:43.373
wait a second,

00:07:43.373 --> 00:07:44.215
you're not ready for this.

00:07:44.215 --> 00:07:46.470
You're not ready for college.

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:49.680
And the instructor in one
of these classes told me

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:52.620
about a student that she had
had a lot of years earlier,

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:54.420
her name was Sarah.

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:55.770
And the instructor could tell

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:58.710
that this student was
really smart and driven

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:00.360
and she wanted a college degree

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.070
because she wanted to be a nurse.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:04.260
But Sarah wasn't gonna be
able to pass the assessment

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:06.967
that was gonna get her out of
this remedial reading class.

00:08:06.967 --> 00:08:08.670
She couldn't read very well at all.

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:10.110
She couldn't write.

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:11.610
And the instructor ended up petitioning

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:13.417
the English Department, saying,

00:08:13.417 --> 00:08:14.557
"Sarah's really bright.

00:08:14.557 --> 00:08:16.807
"She needs a college degree
so she can be a nurse.

00:08:16.807 --> 00:08:18.967
"Let's figure out some way to assess her,

00:08:18.967 --> 00:08:20.857
"get her out of this
class so she can move on

00:08:20.857 --> 00:08:22.620
"and get a degree."

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:23.453
And it worked.

00:08:23.453 --> 00:08:25.380
Sarah passed the class
and then this instructor

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:26.355
whose name is, Jill,

00:08:26.355 --> 00:08:27.840
lost track of Sarah.

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:29.490
She thought about her sometimes.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:31.770
She wondered about what
had happened to her.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:33.030
And then years later,

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:35.010
Jill was in the hospital having surgery

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:37.860
and she woke up and there was Sarah.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:40.650
Sarah was Jill's nurse.

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:42.030
So when I heard this story, I thought,

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:43.380
I really wanna talk to Sarah.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:45.600
So Jill dug up an old email address.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:46.433
I wrote to Sarah.

00:08:46.433 --> 00:08:49.110
Sarah wrote back to me
and in the winter of 2016,

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:51.000
I drove to her house in
Connecticut in the middle

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:53.460
of an ice storm and we
did this long interview,

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:57.420
and Sarah told me all
about her reading problems.

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:00.180
Now, it turns out that Sarah has dyslexia.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:02.100
That was not something
that was ever identified

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:03.060
when she was in school.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:04.230
It actually wasn't anything

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:06.000
that was ever officially diagnosed.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:09.090
It's basically what Sarah
figured out about herself.

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:11.610
And Sarah told me about
all these strategies

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:14.910
that she had to try to get
through the written text.

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:16.290
So there were a certain number of words

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:17.490
that she had just memorize.

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:18.660
She knew what they looked like,

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:21.210
like common words she saw all the time.

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:22.860
And then there were a lot of other words

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:23.970
and she would just guess them.

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:25.560
She would look at the first letter,

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:26.670
look at the last letter,

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:28.920
and think about something that made sense.

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:31.320
And she told me the problem
was she couldn't come up

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:32.880
with a very good guess a lot of times,

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:34.650
so she just skipped a lot of words.

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:38.610
And this way of reading
was really time-consuming.

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:39.930
It was hard. It was frustrating.

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:41.250
It was confusing.

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:43.380
She couldn't focus on the
meaning of what she was reading.

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:45.540
So really, her main strategy
with reading was just

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:46.380
to avoid reading.

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:48.000
She really didn't do it.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:50.310
She had this sister who was
a little bit older than her

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:52.110
who would read things out loud to her

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:53.490
and that's really how
she got through school,

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:55.050
how she got through high school.

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:55.883
Then she got stuck

00:09:55.883 --> 00:09:57.720
in that remedial reading class in college.

00:09:57.720 --> 00:09:59.370
She got saved by her instructor.

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:02.250
She eked her way through
college without opening a book.

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:03.090
She told me, never,

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:06.300
she says she never
opened a book in college.

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:08.550
And her strategy was to
pay really close attention

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:10.080
to the lecture and the class discussion.

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:11.340
She says she has a really good memory,

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:12.270
I believe her,

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:14.820
and that's how she got her nursing degree.

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:17.610
She memorized all the words
that she needs to know

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:18.690
to be able to read and write,

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:20.640
communicate with the
nurses and the doctors

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:22.080
and the medical staff.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:24.570
but she really never learned how to read.

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:26.640
And her story was really amazing to me.

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:29.160
I had all kinds of questions
after talking to her,

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:30.630
about dyslexia first of all.

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:32.100
I knew nothing about it,

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:34.350
about why she was never
identified with dyslexia,

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:36.150
didn't get any help in school.

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:37.980
So this next picture
which you're looking at,

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:39.390
stay on this slide,

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:41.850
I just started to dig into
this question of dyslexia

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:42.990
and learning disabilities.

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:44.760
And there's tons of research about this,

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:46.740
so this is just one little
part of my bookshelf.

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:48.780
These are some of the books
that I started reading.

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:51.720
So I was digging into the
research about dyslexia

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:54.720
and then I was also talking to parents.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:57.630
And I was starting to hear the same story

00:10:57.630 --> 00:10:59.370
from parents all over the country.

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:01.080
First I was talking to parents in Maryland

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:02.040
where I live and I thought,

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:02.873
oh my gosh,

00:11:02.873 --> 00:11:04.680
Maryland has a terrible problem.

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:06.267
And then I was talking
to parents in California

00:11:06.267 --> 00:11:08.460
and Michigan and Florida and, really,

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:11.580
literally, it was the same
story over and over again.

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:13.447
And the story is basically this.

00:11:13.447 --> 00:11:17.227
"Our kid went to school and we
knew something wasn't right.

00:11:17.227 --> 00:11:18.743
"We went to the teacher
and the teacher was like,

00:11:18.743 --> 00:11:21.607
"'Don't worry, make sure you
read lots of books to him.

00:11:21.607 --> 00:11:22.837
"'Everything's gonna be fine.'

00:11:22.837 --> 00:11:23.670
"But we were like,

00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:25.387
"something's not right with reading here.

00:11:25.387 --> 00:11:26.527
"He doesn't seem to get it.

00:11:26.527 --> 00:11:28.110
"He doesn't like it."

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:29.220
This went on for years.

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:30.053
The parents would say,

00:11:30.053 --> 00:11:30.886
"There's something wrong here."

00:11:30.886 --> 00:11:31.747
And the school was saying,

00:11:31.747 --> 00:11:34.417
"Don't worry, we've just gotta
find your kid the right book.

00:11:34.417 --> 00:11:37.590
"He'll get really excited
and it'll all be fine."

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:39.120
And what eventually happens,

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:40.200
these parents were telling me,

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:41.730
if they had time and money,

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:44.190
especially money,
money's a big thing here,

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:46.200
these parents would
pay for private testing

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:48.270
to try to figure out what's
going on with their kid.

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:50.820
And this usually costs
thousands of dollars.

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:52.770
A lot of these parents have
turn to private tutoring,

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:54.510
which is more thousands of dollars.

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:57.990
Sometimes they'd hire a lawyer
or an educational consultant

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:01.380
or both to actually start
a fight with the school

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:03.510
to try to get what their kid needed.

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:05.760
And it's really expensive,
really frustrating,

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:08.340
really hard, really exhausting.

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:11.010
And what happens is the
parents begin to realize

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:13.050
that their child isn't
gonna get what he needs

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:15.660
in public school or he's not
gonna get it fast enough.

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:17.280
'Cause now this kid is like nine,

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:18.510
10 years old.

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:20.220
He hates school.

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:22.470
He can't read very well
so he is falling behind

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:24.060
in other subjects.

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:25.980
Sometimes this kid is
acting out in school,

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:28.590
like maybe this is turned
into a behavior problem

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:31.710
or maybe this is starting
to manifest as anxiety,

00:12:31.710 --> 00:12:33.720
depression, withdrawal.

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:36.450
And this is when the parents tell me,

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:37.710
if they have the resources,

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:38.790
time and money, money,

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:39.690
money, money,

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:41.880
the family pulls their
child out of public school.

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:43.680
So maybe they teach them themself.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:46.890
I've met a whole bunch of
mothers who quit their careers

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:48.360
to teach their children to read,

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:49.830
to homeschool their kids.

00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:51.540
Or maybe they come up with the, literally,

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:52.920
tens of thousands of dollars,

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:56.640
like maybe even $80,000 a year
at some schools around here

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:58.200
that it can cost to send a kid

00:12:58.200 --> 00:12:59.820
to a specialized private school.

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:01.080
And there's a whole bunch
of parts of the country

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:03.270
where there isn't a good private
school to send the kid to,

00:13:03.270 --> 00:13:04.410
even if you could get them in,

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:06.090
even if you did have the money.

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:09.450
So what I started to realize
is that we're in this situation

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:11.910
in this country that if you can come up

00:13:11.910 --> 00:13:13.290
with the money to pay for it,

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:14.910
you can probably find a way for your kid

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:16.470
to be taught how to read.

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:17.337
But if you don't have the money

00:13:17.337 --> 00:13:20.100
and your child is not learning
how to read in school,

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:20.933
what do you do?

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:24.030
So the equity implications
of this are really stunning.

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:25.800
If you're from a low-income family,

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:27.120
even a moderate-income family,

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:28.620
there's really no safety net here.

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:30.150
There's no backup if
you're not being taught

00:13:30.150 --> 00:13:31.530
to read in school.

00:13:31.530 --> 00:13:32.497
One mom said to me,

00:13:32.497 --> 00:13:37.110
"Getting help for a struggling
reader is a rich man's game.

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:38.700
So I was like, whoa,

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:40.080
what, reading?

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:41.940
This is the most basic thing here.

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:44.820
This is the foundation upon
which all learning is built.

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:46.680
So how did that happen?

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:47.880
How is it allowed to continue?

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:48.720
What was going on?

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:50.580
So I did this next reporting project,

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:52.680
you can go to the next slide.

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:56.340
And this is what Alan
mentioned, "Hard Words".

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:59.220
It's an audio documentary
about core reading instruction.

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:01.410
So first I was really
interested in dyslexia,

00:14:01.410 --> 00:14:03.300
what do kids with dyslexia need?

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:05.012
But now I was like, okay,

00:14:05.012 --> 00:14:06.420
let's not think about

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:08.340
what struggling readers
need in particular,

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:09.840
but rather, what do all children need

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:11.400
to learn to become good readers?

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:12.233
What's up with this,

00:14:12.233 --> 00:14:13.950
how do we learn to read?

00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:16.140
And what I discovered is
that there's this huge body

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:17.640
of evidence about reading.

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:20.430
And the bottom line from
that evidence is this,

00:14:20.430 --> 00:14:22.320
decades and decades of research.

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:24.090
What kids with dyslexia need to learn

00:14:24.090 --> 00:14:27.480
to become good readers is
not substantially different

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:30.450
from what all kids need to
learn to become good readers.

00:14:30.450 --> 00:14:32.790
We all need to learn the same things

00:14:32.790 --> 00:14:34.560
to become good readers.

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:38.220
Kids with dyslexia need
a certain intense dose

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:40.110
of a certain kind of instruction.

00:14:40.110 --> 00:14:41.460
But all kids can benefit

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:42.667
from the kind of instruction the kids

00:14:42.667 --> 00:14:45.870
with dyslexia desperately,
desperately need.

00:14:45.870 --> 00:14:48.270
So I started digging into all
this research about reading.

00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:50.130
So you can go to the next slide.

00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:52.650
So this is just a part
of my shelf of books,

00:14:52.650 --> 00:14:54.443
the slide before this.

00:14:54.443 --> 00:14:57.300
This is just questions
about how do we read?

00:14:57.300 --> 00:14:59.281
How do our brains learn to do that?

00:14:59.281 --> 00:15:00.540
How do we learn?

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:01.620
You know, what do we need to know?

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:02.700
What do we need to be taught?

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:04.230
Why do some people struggle with reading?

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:06.540
Why is learning to read
really hard for some people?

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:07.373
Because it is,

00:15:07.373 --> 00:15:09.210
it's really hard for some people.

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:10.680
And a lot is known about this,

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:13.110
a lot is known about reading
and why kids struggle.

00:15:13.110 --> 00:15:14.700
And there's actually research that shows

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:17.610
that different reading methods
affect reading development.

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:20.310
So different ways of
teaching kids to read,

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:22.350
you can actually see it in their brain.

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:23.730
So you can go to the next one.

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:25.770
This is a book that taught me so much

00:15:25.770 --> 00:15:27.397
and I highly recommend this book,

00:15:27.397 --> 00:15:28.290
"Reading in the Brain".

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:29.250
It's not light.

00:15:29.250 --> 00:15:30.083
You can see,

00:15:30.083 --> 00:15:32.700
I don't think I've ever put so
many sticky notes in a book.

00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:34.650
And there's a big takeaway from this book

00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:36.510
and from all this research.

00:15:36.510 --> 00:15:38.430
And maybe it seems kind of obvious

00:15:38.430 --> 00:15:40.320
but it wasn't obvious to me,

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:43.920
is that our brain is actually
the product of millions

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:47.550
of years of evolution in a
world without writing, right.

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:49.200
So as human beings,

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:50.460
we just didn't do this reading

00:15:50.460 --> 00:15:52.050
and writing thing for a long time,

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:54.420
so our brains actually
aren't wired to do it.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:56.790
We're not born with brains
that are wired to read.

00:15:56.790 --> 00:15:58.710
It turns out that some of us don't need

00:15:58.710 --> 00:16:00.300
to be taught that much,

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:02.490
we don't need very much instruction,

00:16:02.490 --> 00:16:04.350
but some of us need a lot.

00:16:04.350 --> 00:16:06.240
So you can go to the next slide.

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:08.640
This is really the book
that sort of blew my mind

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:09.473
about all of this.

00:16:09.473 --> 00:16:12.900
It was written in 2017 by
a guy named Mark Seidenberg

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:16.470
who's a cognitive scientist at
the University of Wisconsin.

00:16:16.470 --> 00:16:18.990
And it made me think a
whole lot about a lot

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:22.620
of assumptions that I didn't
realize I had about reading.

00:16:22.620 --> 00:16:25.507
And here's a quote from the
book by Mark Seidenberg.

00:16:25.507 --> 00:16:30.337
"Reading to children rarely
involves teaching them to read.

00:16:30.337 --> 00:16:33.727
"The activity contributes
to the child's progress,

00:16:33.727 --> 00:16:36.397
"but it is not all that is required.

00:16:36.397 --> 00:16:40.147
"Some children do learn to
read with minimal instruction,

00:16:40.147 --> 00:16:42.787
"but unlike learning to talk,

00:16:42.787 --> 00:16:45.597
"it is a mistake to
assume that they will."

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:47.977
Another quote from this book is,

00:16:47.977 --> 00:16:49.477
"Children who are read to,

00:16:49.477 --> 00:16:52.080
"Until the Cow Jumps Over the Moon",

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:55.173
can still have difficulty
becoming readers.

00:16:56.070 --> 00:16:57.900
So this book is about that,

00:16:57.900 --> 00:17:01.380
and it's also about politics and policy

00:17:01.380 --> 00:17:04.560
and teacher preparation
and what teachers learn,

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:07.350
and really, more like
what they don't learn

00:17:07.350 --> 00:17:10.200
about how kids learn to
read and how to teach it.

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:12.510
It goes into the reading wars

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:15.090
and ideas about how kids learn to read,

00:17:15.090 --> 00:17:16.830
theories about it that we've been fighting

00:17:16.830 --> 00:17:18.660
about for centuries.

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:20.880
The "Sold a Story" podcast that I talked

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:21.750
to you about before,

00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:24.060
at one point we went way back in history,

00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:26.700
actually, we have a timeline
that starts in the 1600s.

00:17:26.700 --> 00:17:27.533
And at one point,

00:17:27.533 --> 00:17:28.590
we had this whole section in there

00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:31.593
where we talked about the
reading wars back in the 1800s,

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:35.040
and they were just drop-down,

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:37.170
horrible, bitter fights.

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:38.940
And they really started
'cause this guy named,

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:40.800
Horace Mann, who you've probably heard of,

00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:42.750
who's like the father of public education,

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:46.050
was the first Secretary of
Education in Massachusetts.

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:47.970
And he came along with this idea,

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:49.920
he believed in this particular method

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:52.320
about teaching kids to read,

00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:55.260
and it was called the Whole-Word Method.

00:17:55.260 --> 00:17:56.790
And Horace Mann believed this.

00:17:56.790 --> 00:17:58.687
These are quotes from Horace Mann.

00:17:58.687 --> 00:18:00.607
"Children learn to read by being

00:18:00.607 --> 00:18:02.760
"in the presence of books."

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:04.297
And then he said this,

00:18:04.297 --> 00:18:07.050
"I am satisfied that our greatest error,"

00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:08.017
this is Horace Mann,

00:18:08.017 --> 00:18:10.297
"our greatest error in teaching children

00:18:10.297 --> 00:18:13.777
"to read lies in beginning
with the alphabet.

00:18:13.777 --> 00:18:16.567
"No thorough reform will ever be affected

00:18:16.567 --> 00:18:20.880
"in our schools until this
practice is abolished."

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:23.730
So Horace Mann did not like the idea

00:18:23.730 --> 00:18:26.160
that kids were gonna be
taught about the letters

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:28.410
and the sounds that make
up the words and language.

00:18:28.410 --> 00:18:30.450
He thought that was gonna
be tedious and boring

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:32.340
and that it was unnecessary.

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:35.730
In fact, this is one of my
favorite quotes from Horace Mann.

00:18:35.730 --> 00:18:39.367
He said, "In regard to all
the other sources of pleasure,

00:18:39.367 --> 00:18:40.987
"beauty, motion,

00:18:40.987 --> 00:18:42.937
"music, memory,

00:18:42.937 --> 00:18:46.807
"the alphabetic column
presents an utter blank.

00:18:46.807 --> 00:18:50.107
"There stands in silence and death,

00:18:50.107 --> 00:18:53.437
"the stiff, perpendicular
row of characters,

00:18:53.437 --> 00:18:56.527
"lank, stark, immovable.

00:18:56.527 --> 00:18:58.297
"They are skeleton-shaped,

00:18:58.297 --> 00:19:01.327
"bloodless, ghostly apparitions.

00:19:01.327 --> 00:19:05.077
"And hence it is no wonder
that the children look

00:19:05.077 --> 00:19:10.020
"and feel so deathlike when
compelled to face them."

00:19:10.020 --> 00:19:11.700
So I think it's important
to recognize that one

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:14.460
of the founders of public
education felt very strongly

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:15.810
about how to teach kids to read,

00:19:15.810 --> 00:19:18.750
and he really thought it
was gonna be a bad idea

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:20.130
to start with letters and sounds,

00:19:20.130 --> 00:19:21.360
to start with phonics.

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:23.640
He thought that was gonna be bad for kids.

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:26.310
And then, the Boston School masters,

00:19:26.310 --> 00:19:27.840
and this guy named Samuel Green,

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:28.710
who is the principal

00:19:28.710 --> 00:19:30.993
of the Phillips Grammar School in Boston,

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:33.210
were listening to Horace Mann

00:19:33.210 --> 00:19:35.077
and reading things he was
writing and they're like,

00:19:35.077 --> 00:19:35.910
"Whoa, whoa,

00:19:35.910 --> 00:19:36.743
"wait a minute, Horace,

00:19:36.743 --> 00:19:38.220
"hold your horses."

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:40.350
And they wrote this retort to Horace Mann,

00:19:40.350 --> 00:19:41.310
which is fascinating,

00:19:41.310 --> 00:19:43.530
in their very 19th century way.

00:19:43.530 --> 00:19:45.907
And they wrote things like this:

00:19:45.907 --> 00:19:49.897
"Reading, justly deserves the
first rank among the studies

00:19:49.897 --> 00:19:51.427
"of our schools.

00:19:51.427 --> 00:19:53.317
"As an accomplishment alone,

00:19:53.317 --> 00:19:56.257
"it possesses intrinsic excellence;

00:19:56.257 --> 00:19:58.147
"but considered as fundamental

00:19:58.147 --> 00:19:59.797
"to other departments of learning;

00:19:59.797 --> 00:20:03.180
"its value cannot be
too highly estimated."

00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:04.013
So they were saying,

00:20:04.013 --> 00:20:04.987
"Hold on, Horace,

00:20:04.987 --> 00:20:07.387
"you are talking about
something critical here.

00:20:07.387 --> 00:20:09.367
"You're coming up with this
new idea to teach kids to read

00:20:09.367 --> 00:20:11.610
"and we don't think that
this is a good idea."

00:20:11.610 --> 00:20:14.347
They said, "In judging
the merits of any system

00:20:14.347 --> 00:20:16.207
"by which reading may be taught,

00:20:16.207 --> 00:20:18.967
"remote, as well as an immediate effects

00:20:18.967 --> 00:20:20.887
"should be duly regarded.

00:20:20.887 --> 00:20:24.517
"A child, even at the
threshold of his education,

00:20:24.517 --> 00:20:27.307
"should not be subjected to any delay,

00:20:27.307 --> 00:20:31.147
"which the formation of
correct habits may require.

00:20:31.147 --> 00:20:33.397
"He should never be
hurried over difficulties,

00:20:33.397 --> 00:20:34.957
"at first concealed,

00:20:34.957 --> 00:20:36.247
"yet, in his progress,

00:20:36.247 --> 00:20:39.277
"unavoidable, simply to make his entrance

00:20:39.277 --> 00:20:41.107
"into the temple of learning,

00:20:41.107 --> 00:20:43.117
"easy and agreeable.

00:20:43.117 --> 00:20:47.227
"A system of instruction is
subjected to an unworthy test,

00:20:47.227 --> 00:20:50.587
"when the chief excellence
claimed for it consists

00:20:50.587 --> 00:20:53.790
"in smoothing the path of the learner."

00:20:53.790 --> 00:20:55.417
So essentially, Horace Mann was saying,

00:20:55.417 --> 00:20:57.907
"Let's make this easier for kids to learn

00:20:57.907 --> 00:20:59.977
"to read by not focusing
on the letters and sounds

00:20:59.977 --> 00:21:01.117
"and the stuff that's hard.

00:21:01.117 --> 00:21:02.437
"English spelling is hard.

00:21:02.437 --> 00:21:04.927
"Let's just get kids into books and words

00:21:04.927 --> 00:21:07.440
"and get them right there to the end."

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:09.517
And the Boston School masters are saying,

00:21:09.517 --> 00:21:10.657
"Wait a second,

00:21:10.657 --> 00:21:12.637
"you're gonna try to make
this easy at the beginning

00:21:12.637 --> 00:21:13.897
"by just getting kids into books

00:21:13.897 --> 00:21:16.297
"and not teaching them letters and sounds,

00:21:16.297 --> 00:21:18.221
"putting the hard stuff on the side,

00:21:18.221 --> 00:21:21.510
"but you are going to damage
those children in the end."

00:21:21.510 --> 00:21:23.160
And no one could agree.

00:21:23.160 --> 00:21:26.430
Should you start with the way
that Horace Mann was saying,

00:21:26.430 --> 00:21:27.990
sort of meaning first, whole words,

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:30.060
whole sentences, whole stories,

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:32.370
or should you start
with letters and sounds,

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:33.630
a sort of bottom-up approach,

00:21:33.630 --> 00:21:35.730
emphasized decoding, right?

00:21:35.730 --> 00:21:38.610
And we fought about this forever
because no one really knew.

00:21:38.610 --> 00:21:41.580
But then, all this research
started being done,

00:21:41.580 --> 00:21:43.860
sort of exploded in the 1970s,

00:21:43.860 --> 00:21:45.630
motivated by the question,

00:21:45.630 --> 00:21:47.040
how do kids learn to read?

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:48.750
Like seriously, how does that work?

00:21:48.750 --> 00:21:50.760
So you can go to the next slide.

00:21:50.760 --> 00:21:52.724
And this is something I talk about a lot.

00:21:52.724 --> 00:21:56.610
I think it's a really good place
to begin because it really,

00:21:56.610 --> 00:21:59.730
really helped me understand
how reading works.

00:21:59.730 --> 00:22:02.400
It's called the Simple View of Reading.

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:04.230
And the Simple View of
Reading does not say

00:22:04.230 --> 00:22:06.660
that reading is simple, really important.

00:22:06.660 --> 00:22:07.493
To the contrary,

00:22:07.493 --> 00:22:10.020
reading is actually really
complicated, it's complex.

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:12.240
But the Simple View of
Reading is a simple model

00:22:12.240 --> 00:22:14.400
to understand a complex process.

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:16.050
So it was first proposed a long time ago.

00:22:16.050 --> 00:22:16.883
I was in high school.

00:22:16.883 --> 00:22:18.330
It was 1986.

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:20.070
And this model was actually proposed

00:22:20.070 --> 00:22:22.560
because these researchers were
trying to clarify the role

00:22:22.560 --> 00:22:24.120
of decoding and reading comprehension,

00:22:24.120 --> 00:22:26.610
because everyone was fighting about this.

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:28.470
This was sort of when the reading wars

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:31.173
of the late 20th century
were getting into full swing.

00:22:32.190 --> 00:22:34.020
Now, everyone agrees that
the goal of reading is

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:35.400
to comprehend text.

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:36.267
Everyone agrees on that.

00:22:36.267 --> 00:22:37.350
And the question is,

00:22:37.350 --> 00:22:38.850
how does a little kid get there?

00:22:38.850 --> 00:22:40.410
So the Simple View says that,

00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:43.800
reading comprehension is the
product of two things, right.

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:47.130
So one is your ability to
decode the words, right.

00:22:47.130 --> 00:22:48.090
And the other part

00:22:48.090 --> 00:22:50.400
of the equation is your
language comprehension.

00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:53.520
So that's your ability to
understand spoken language.

00:22:53.520 --> 00:22:55.800
So we're not talking about
your ability to read text.

00:22:55.800 --> 00:22:59.310
Language comprehension is your
ability to understand meaning

00:22:59.310 --> 00:23:00.390
when someone is talking

00:23:00.390 --> 00:23:02.670
or when text is being
read out loud to you.

00:23:02.670 --> 00:23:03.503
So, for example,

00:23:03.503 --> 00:23:04.336
when someone says to you,

00:23:04.336 --> 00:23:05.940
she is reading the book,

00:23:05.940 --> 00:23:07.440
you know what the verb
means in that sentence,

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:08.730
you know what she's doing.

00:23:08.730 --> 00:23:10.440
So what the Simple View
says is that if you

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:12.810
have really good language
comprehension skills,

00:23:12.810 --> 00:23:14.760
but zero decoding skills,

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:17.010
your reading comprehension will be zero,

00:23:17.010 --> 00:23:19.230
because zero times anything is zero.

00:23:19.230 --> 00:23:20.910
And the Simple View also says that if you

00:23:20.910 --> 00:23:22.560
have really good decoding skills

00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:25.170
but very poor language
comprehension skills,

00:23:25.170 --> 00:23:26.730
you just don't know the meaning

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:28.740
of that many words in spoken language,

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:31.440
your reading comprehension
will not be very good either.

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:34.050
So, here's why this is important.

00:23:34.050 --> 00:23:36.840
Most kids entering school have very little

00:23:36.840 --> 00:23:39.570
when it comes to the decoding
part of the equation, right.

00:23:39.570 --> 00:23:42.930
They have zero or close to
zero when it comes to the D

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:44.850
in the Simple View of Reading.

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:47.130
But they do have something when it comes

00:23:47.130 --> 00:23:49.470
to the language comprehension
part of the equation.

00:23:49.470 --> 00:23:51.210
So when kids enter school,

00:23:51.210 --> 00:23:52.620
they know the meaning of lots of words,

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:55.080
if they're English speakers in America,

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:57.990
but they don't know how to
decode those words yet, right.

00:23:57.990 --> 00:24:00.600
So this is why people familiar
with the science of reading,

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:03.380
so-called, call for an emphasis
on decoding instruction

00:24:03.380 --> 00:24:04.650
in the early grades.

00:24:04.650 --> 00:24:05.700
Because if the goal,

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:07.440
if the end goal here is to try

00:24:07.440 --> 00:24:08.730
to get your reading comprehension,

00:24:08.730 --> 00:24:10.290
and you have a bunch of
five and six-year-olds

00:24:10.290 --> 00:24:11.760
who are sitting in front of you,

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:14.010
they have language comprehension skills,

00:24:14.010 --> 00:24:16.740
but they really have
virtually no decoding skills,

00:24:16.740 --> 00:24:19.290
you need to help those children
develop decoding skills.

00:24:19.290 --> 00:24:20.790
But, and this is so important,

00:24:20.790 --> 00:24:23.220
the Simple View clearly
shows that focusing only

00:24:23.220 --> 00:24:24.570
on decoding would be a very,

00:24:24.570 --> 00:24:27.810
very big mistake because it's
only part of the equation.

00:24:27.810 --> 00:24:29.820
And, this is really important,

00:24:29.820 --> 00:24:30.720
kids come into school

00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:33.810
with very different language
comprehension skills, right.

00:24:33.810 --> 00:24:36.570
Some kids know the meaning
of lots and lots of words.

00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:39.210
Some kids are far smaller vocabularies.

00:24:39.210 --> 00:24:42.420
Some kids are speaking
little or no English.

00:24:42.420 --> 00:24:45.150
So reading instruction that
aligns with the Simple View has

00:24:45.150 --> 00:24:47.070
to focus on this language
comprehension part

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:48.660
of the equation too.

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:50.490
So I was in this first grade classroom

00:24:50.490 --> 00:24:52.650
where reading instruction
was deliberately aligned

00:24:52.650 --> 00:24:53.670
with the Simple View,

00:24:53.670 --> 00:24:56.730
and what I saw was phonics
instruction in one part

00:24:56.730 --> 00:24:57.563
of the reading instruction,

00:24:57.563 --> 00:24:59.460
and the kids were broken
into small groups,

00:24:59.460 --> 00:25:01.920
depending on the level
of their decoding skills.

00:25:01.920 --> 00:25:04.500
And then in another part
of the reading instruction,

00:25:04.500 --> 00:25:06.600
they were getting explicit
vocabulary lessons

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:09.420
and lots of reading out
loud by the teacher.

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:11.220
And the words that the kids
had learned were posted

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:12.900
on cards all over the classroom.

00:25:12.900 --> 00:25:13.920
They were words like,

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:16.740
extraordinary and
ridiculous and neighborly,

00:25:16.740 --> 00:25:18.480
which are not words that the vast majority

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:20.370
of first graders are
gonna be able to decode.

00:25:20.370 --> 00:25:21.960
They shouldn't be expected to.

00:25:21.960 --> 00:25:23.220
But the first graders in this class

00:25:23.220 --> 00:25:24.810
were learning the
pronunciation and meaning

00:25:24.810 --> 00:25:27.060
of those words so that when
they're able to decode them,

00:25:27.060 --> 00:25:28.770
they'll know what the words mean.

00:25:28.770 --> 00:25:30.090
And this is really important,

00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:33.180
every single child in
that class spoke English

00:25:33.180 --> 00:25:34.680
as a second language,

00:25:34.680 --> 00:25:35.513
and for some kids,

00:25:35.513 --> 00:25:37.380
English was their third language.

00:25:37.380 --> 00:25:41.610
So, this Simple View was
proposed as a model back in 1986,

00:25:41.610 --> 00:25:43.620
and then the basics of this model

00:25:43.620 --> 00:25:46.740
had been confirmed over and
over again by research since.

00:25:46.740 --> 00:25:47.910
And I think it's really helpful

00:25:47.910 --> 00:25:49.530
'cause it disentangles some of the stuff

00:25:49.530 --> 00:25:51.450
that's really contentious
in the debates about reading

00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:52.950
and has been for decades,

00:25:52.950 --> 00:25:54.360
for centuries, right.

00:25:54.360 --> 00:25:56.310
So what's known as the whole word,

00:25:56.310 --> 00:25:58.440
which sort of became the whole language,

00:25:58.440 --> 00:26:01.230
which is now more recently
called balanced literacy.

00:26:01.230 --> 00:26:04.440
So in the whole language
balanced literacy view,

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:05.790
the focus right from the start

00:26:05.790 --> 00:26:08.040
of reading instruction
should be on getting kids

00:26:08.040 --> 00:26:09.780
to focus on the meaning
of what they're reading.

00:26:09.780 --> 00:26:11.370
That's what Horace Mann wanted.

00:26:11.370 --> 00:26:13.080
Whole language and balanced literacy

00:26:13.080 --> 00:26:15.870
are meaning emphasis approaches
to reading instruction.

00:26:15.870 --> 00:26:18.630
Get kids to focus on
the whole, the meaning.

00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:21.600
As opposed to what's known
as a code emphasis approach,

00:26:21.600 --> 00:26:23.640
which emphasizes decoding
skills at the beginning

00:26:23.640 --> 00:26:25.140
of reading instruction, right.

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:27.270
So early reading instruction that aligns

00:26:27.270 --> 00:26:30.120
with the scientific research
over the past 50 years,

00:26:30.120 --> 00:26:32.670
is a code emphasis approach not only,

00:26:32.670 --> 00:26:35.640
but emphasizing so that
kids can get to meaning,

00:26:35.640 --> 00:26:37.770
because everyone agrees
that meaning is the goal.

00:26:37.770 --> 00:26:38.970
And the question again is,

00:26:38.970 --> 00:26:41.100
how does a little kid get there?

00:26:41.100 --> 00:26:42.480
So, in my reporting,

00:26:42.480 --> 00:26:45.060
what I became very interested
in trying to figure out is,

00:26:45.060 --> 00:26:48.510
how are kids being taught in school?

00:26:48.510 --> 00:26:50.130
That's what I started to dig into next.

00:26:50.130 --> 00:26:52.020
So let me just tell you a
little bit about what I learned.

00:26:52.020 --> 00:26:53.880
You can go to the next slide.

00:26:53.880 --> 00:26:55.800
This is just one little artifact here.

00:26:55.800 --> 00:26:58.440
It was sent to me by a
mom in Massachusetts.

00:26:58.440 --> 00:27:00.780
It came home in her son's backpack.

00:27:00.780 --> 00:27:03.270
And these are all
strategies for kids to use

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:05.700
when they're learning
to read and they come

00:27:05.700 --> 00:27:07.350
to a word that they don't know.

00:27:07.350 --> 00:27:08.580
And here's the thing,

00:27:08.580 --> 00:27:11.130
these strategies make sense.

00:27:11.130 --> 00:27:13.260
They seem totally sensible.

00:27:13.260 --> 00:27:14.700
You get to a word you don't know,

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:15.780
what can you do?

00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:17.040
Well, you can look at the picture

00:27:17.040 --> 00:27:19.170
to try to figure out
what the word might be.

00:27:19.170 --> 00:27:20.430
You don't wanna completely guess,

00:27:20.430 --> 00:27:21.930
so you can look at the first letter,

00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:23.730
like Sarah did, for example.

00:27:23.730 --> 00:27:24.990
You can look at some of the letters.

00:27:24.990 --> 00:27:26.310
It's gonna narrow your choices,

00:27:26.310 --> 00:27:27.810
help you come up with a good guess.

00:27:27.810 --> 00:27:28.860
You can then check,

00:27:28.860 --> 00:27:30.060
you can see if you were right.

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:31.470
Reread the sentence using the word,

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:32.730
see if the sentence makes sense.

00:27:32.730 --> 00:27:34.140
Ah, maybe you were right.

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:35.250
If you're really stuck,

00:27:35.250 --> 00:27:36.840
you can just skip the word and move on.

00:27:36.840 --> 00:27:39.780
Skip, hopefully you can get the
gist of the sentence anyway.

00:27:39.780 --> 00:27:41.790
So what I was trying to understand is,

00:27:41.790 --> 00:27:44.220
I was seeing stuff like
this all over the place,

00:27:44.220 --> 00:27:46.980
like on postcards, on bookmarks,

00:27:46.980 --> 00:27:48.720
there are these little cartoon characters

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:51.000
that have versions of these strategies.

00:27:51.000 --> 00:27:53.370
This was everywhere in American schools.

00:27:53.370 --> 00:27:55.440
I was seeing it in classrooms
that I was visiting.

00:27:55.440 --> 00:27:56.430
I was getting sent it,

00:27:56.430 --> 00:27:58.290
parents were telling me
about it and I was like,

00:27:58.290 --> 00:28:00.390
what is this about?

00:28:00.390 --> 00:28:03.270
What is the idea about how reading works

00:28:03.270 --> 00:28:05.760
that these strategies are based on?

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:07.590
That's what I wanted to get interested in

00:28:07.590 --> 00:28:09.360
and that's what I started
to try to figure out.

00:28:09.360 --> 00:28:11.610
So you can go to the next slide.

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:15.030
It turns out that these
ideas are based in a theory,

00:28:15.030 --> 00:28:19.230
a theory about reading that's
been around for a long time,

00:28:19.230 --> 00:28:22.050
that's come to be known sort
of more recently as cueing.

00:28:22.050 --> 00:28:23.760
Not everyone uses that term.

00:28:23.760 --> 00:28:26.400
But the idea is that
readers use different kinds

00:28:26.400 --> 00:28:29.310
of information or cues or clues

00:28:29.310 --> 00:28:31.260
to identify the words as they're reading.

00:28:31.260 --> 00:28:33.300
And this was sort of articulated

00:28:33.300 --> 00:28:36.120
as a theory back in the 1960s,

00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:37.110
and it was sort of a big deal.

00:28:37.110 --> 00:28:38.640
It was a big deal in the research area

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:40.260
when this theory came out.

00:28:40.260 --> 00:28:42.609
And the basic idea behind
this cueing theory,

00:28:42.609 --> 00:28:44.580
remember this is the 1960s,

00:28:44.580 --> 00:28:46.440
we really didn't know how people read yet,

00:28:46.440 --> 00:28:48.900
the theory here is that readers don't have

00:28:48.900 --> 00:28:51.030
to sound out written words
to know what they are.

00:28:51.030 --> 00:28:52.140
They can, right,

00:28:52.140 --> 00:28:53.490
they can sound out the words,

00:28:53.490 --> 00:28:57.630
but they don't have to because
they can use other strategies

00:28:57.630 --> 00:28:59.010
to figure out the words.

00:28:59.010 --> 00:29:02.070
They can do things like look
at the first letter of a word,

00:29:02.070 --> 00:29:03.150
use the context,

00:29:03.150 --> 00:29:04.380
think of a word that makes sense,

00:29:04.380 --> 00:29:05.490
look at the pictures.

00:29:05.490 --> 00:29:08.910
They can essentially predict
the words as they're reading.

00:29:08.910 --> 00:29:10.680
The problem is,

00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:13.050
this is not the way that
skilled readers do it.

00:29:13.050 --> 00:29:14.850
This is not the way that
skilled readers read.

00:29:14.850 --> 00:29:16.450
So you can go to the next slide.

00:29:17.460 --> 00:29:20.610
I did this reporting project
in 2019 where I was just,

00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:23.850
I got really interested
in this cueing theory,

00:29:23.850 --> 00:29:27.420
and how this theory and the
word-reading strategies based

00:29:27.420 --> 00:29:31.680
on this theory became foundational
in reading curriculum,

00:29:31.680 --> 00:29:34.890
reading assessments and
reading intervention programs

00:29:34.890 --> 00:29:37.710
that are very popular in American schools

00:29:37.710 --> 00:29:40.320
that say they use a
balanced literacy approach

00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:41.580
to teaching reading.

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:43.560
And surveys show that most schools,

00:29:43.560 --> 00:29:45.180
at least until recently,

00:29:45.180 --> 00:29:47.883
said they used a balanced
literacy approach.

00:29:48.870 --> 00:29:50.790
Cueing is at the foundation

00:29:50.790 --> 00:29:52.410
of that balanced literacy approach,

00:29:52.410 --> 00:29:55.680
but this cueing theory
as I said, isn't right.

00:29:55.680 --> 00:29:58.320
It's not how skilled reading works.

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:00.360
Cognitive scientists showed this back

00:30:00.360 --> 00:30:02.370
in the 1970s and '80s.

00:30:02.370 --> 00:30:04.050
Skilled readers, when
you're a good reader,

00:30:04.050 --> 00:30:06.060
you do not use cues and context

00:30:06.060 --> 00:30:07.470
to identify words as you're reading.

00:30:07.470 --> 00:30:10.320
In fact, this is the amazing thing,

00:30:10.320 --> 00:30:12.570
what scientists figured
out is that this is

00:30:12.570 --> 00:30:14.850
how poor readers read, right.

00:30:14.850 --> 00:30:17.850
Struggling readers often have a hard time

00:30:17.850 --> 00:30:19.800
with word identification.

00:30:19.800 --> 00:30:22.020
Too many of the words that
they come across are kind

00:30:22.020 --> 00:30:23.460
of like little mysteries,

00:30:23.460 --> 00:30:25.650
little series of letters
that they don't know

00:30:25.650 --> 00:30:27.150
and they can't quite figure out.

00:30:27.150 --> 00:30:28.980
So, like Sarah,

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:30.720
they have all these other
strategies that they use

00:30:30.720 --> 00:30:32.910
to try to figure out what
the words say, right.

00:30:32.910 --> 00:30:35.130
And often, they don't like reading,

00:30:35.130 --> 00:30:36.799
they just get a gist of the reading.

00:30:36.799 --> 00:30:40.230
They guess and skip and use context.

00:30:40.230 --> 00:30:41.460
This is not what reading is like

00:30:41.460 --> 00:30:42.990
when you're a skilled reader.

00:30:42.990 --> 00:30:45.060
What cognitive scientists
have figured out is

00:30:45.060 --> 00:30:47.100
that this really is the key difference

00:30:47.100 --> 00:30:49.400
between skilled readers
and unskilled readers.

00:30:50.850 --> 00:30:52.200
Skilled readers can immediately

00:30:52.200 --> 00:30:53.700
and accurately recognize words.

00:30:53.700 --> 00:30:57.870
They don't need to guess or
predict or use the context.

00:30:57.870 --> 00:30:58.890
And skilled readers,

00:30:58.890 --> 00:31:00.030
once you're a skilled reader,

00:31:00.030 --> 00:31:01.110
if you're a skilled reader,

00:31:01.110 --> 00:31:04.170
you actually know tens
of thousands of words

00:31:04.170 --> 00:31:05.130
in a split second,

00:31:05.130 --> 00:31:06.900
like instantly on sight.

00:31:06.900 --> 00:31:09.030
In fact, if you're a skilled reader,

00:31:09.030 --> 00:31:11.490
your brain has gotten
so good at reading words

00:31:11.490 --> 00:31:15.000
that you process the word
book faster than you process

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:16.350
a picture of a book,

00:31:16.350 --> 00:31:18.210
which is really amazing 'cause remember,

00:31:18.210 --> 00:31:20.130
your brain was not designed to do it.

00:31:20.130 --> 00:31:22.290
So how did your brain get so good at that?

00:31:22.290 --> 00:31:23.880
So I'm just gonna take
another couple of minutes

00:31:23.880 --> 00:31:25.620
to explain another couple key concepts

00:31:25.620 --> 00:31:27.390
and then we're gonna move on to questions.

00:31:27.390 --> 00:31:28.223
But it happens

00:31:28.223 --> 00:31:31.260
through this process called
orthographic mapping.

00:31:31.260 --> 00:31:32.430
And I think it's really key

00:31:32.430 --> 00:31:35.100
to understand a little bit
about orthographic mapping

00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:36.960
to understand why teaching kids

00:31:36.960 --> 00:31:39.150
how to decode words is so important,

00:31:39.150 --> 00:31:41.910
why phonics is important, and,

00:31:41.910 --> 00:31:45.540
why teaching kids the cueing
strategies is not a good idea.

00:31:45.540 --> 00:31:48.690
So yes to phonics and no
to the cueing strategies.

00:31:48.690 --> 00:31:50.970
So here's a quick and
simplified explanation

00:31:50.970 --> 00:31:52.350
of orthographic mapping.

00:31:52.350 --> 00:31:54.360
It's basically the process that we use

00:31:54.360 --> 00:31:57.090
to store printed words
in our long-term memory.

00:31:57.090 --> 00:31:58.890
And here's how you do it.

00:31:58.890 --> 00:32:02.430
You attend closely to how
a written word is spelled

00:32:02.430 --> 00:32:04.560
and you link that sequence of letters

00:32:04.560 --> 00:32:06.480
to the word's pronunciation and meaning.

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:08.700
So it's three things, the spelling,

00:32:08.700 --> 00:32:10.620
the pronunciation, and the meaning.

00:32:10.620 --> 00:32:12.480
So for a very simple example,

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:14.430
a little kid knows the
meaning and pronunciation

00:32:14.430 --> 00:32:16.080
of the word, cat.

00:32:16.080 --> 00:32:17.610
And the written word, cat,

00:32:17.610 --> 00:32:19.620
gets orthographically mapped to her memory

00:32:19.620 --> 00:32:22.110
when she links the sounds, at,

00:32:22.110 --> 00:32:24.180
to the written word, C-A-T.

00:32:24.180 --> 00:32:25.740
So that requires an awareness

00:32:25.740 --> 00:32:27.270
of the speech sounds and words,

00:32:27.270 --> 00:32:29.220
that's called phonemic awareness.

00:32:29.220 --> 00:32:30.360
It requires an understanding

00:32:30.360 --> 00:32:31.560
of how those sounds are represented

00:32:31.560 --> 00:32:32.820
by letters, that's phonics.

00:32:32.820 --> 00:32:35.490
So you need good phonics
and phonemic awareness

00:32:35.490 --> 00:32:38.670
to orthographically map
words into your memory.

00:32:38.670 --> 00:32:40.710
And once a word has been
orthographically mapped

00:32:40.710 --> 00:32:41.543
to your memory,

00:32:41.543 --> 00:32:42.840
you know it instantly on sight.

00:32:42.840 --> 00:32:46.020
In fact, you cannot suppress your ability

00:32:46.020 --> 00:32:48.030
to read the word, it's fascinating.

00:32:48.030 --> 00:32:50.490
You don't have to consciously
sound out the word

00:32:50.490 --> 00:32:51.780
when you see it.

00:32:51.780 --> 00:32:54.090
But you know the word in a split second

00:32:54.090 --> 00:32:56.280
because at some point,

00:32:56.280 --> 00:32:59.250
maybe when you were like eight
years old or five years old,

00:32:59.250 --> 00:33:02.220
at some point you successfully
sounded the word out

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:04.320
and you link the spelling
of the word in your mind

00:33:04.320 --> 00:33:06.990
with the meaning and the
pronunciation of that word.

00:33:06.990 --> 00:33:08.700
So by about second grade,

00:33:08.700 --> 00:33:12.450
a typically developing reader
who has good phonic skills

00:33:12.450 --> 00:33:14.490
needs just a few exposures to a word

00:33:14.490 --> 00:33:15.630
through its pronunciation,

00:33:15.630 --> 00:33:18.210
its spelling and its meaning, and bam,

00:33:18.210 --> 00:33:20.010
the word is mapped to her memory.

00:33:20.010 --> 00:33:22.710
The more words that you map
to your memory this way,

00:33:22.710 --> 00:33:24.120
the more you can focus on the meaning

00:33:24.120 --> 00:33:25.440
of what you're reading.

00:33:25.440 --> 00:33:27.840
You're not using your brain
power to identify the words,

00:33:27.840 --> 00:33:29.700
you're using your brain
power to understand

00:33:29.700 --> 00:33:30.533
what you're reading.

00:33:30.533 --> 00:33:31.830
And that's the goal, right,

00:33:31.830 --> 00:33:34.620
for readers to understand
what they read or are reading.

00:33:34.620 --> 00:33:37.050
But, when teachers use that cueing system,

00:33:37.050 --> 00:33:39.420
when they teach all those
word-reading strategies,

00:33:39.420 --> 00:33:43.950
they're actually impeding that
orthographic mapping process.

00:33:43.950 --> 00:33:45.210
So you can go to the next slide.

00:33:45.210 --> 00:33:47.010
I'm just gonna tell you
this one little story

00:33:47.010 --> 00:33:48.180
and then wrap up.

00:33:48.180 --> 00:33:51.600
So these are first graders
in Oakland, California,

00:33:51.600 --> 00:33:53.970
and a literacy coach who
worked with these girls came

00:33:53.970 --> 00:33:56.190
to see that teaching the cueing strategies

00:33:56.190 --> 00:33:59.460
was actually making it harder
for many of her students

00:33:59.460 --> 00:34:00.900
to learn how to read.

00:34:00.900 --> 00:34:02.850
The coach's name is Margaret Goldberg.

00:34:02.850 --> 00:34:05.400
She was hired by the Oakland
Unified School District back

00:34:05.400 --> 00:34:08.430
in 2015 to teach a program called,

00:34:08.430 --> 00:34:10.860
Level Literacy Intervention.

00:34:10.860 --> 00:34:13.230
LLI is a reading intervention program

00:34:13.230 --> 00:34:16.200
that does include some
phonics instruction.

00:34:16.200 --> 00:34:17.610
Because remember, one of the cues

00:34:17.610 --> 00:34:20.490
for figuring out a word
is to use the letters.

00:34:20.490 --> 00:34:23.280
But LLI is rooted in that idea,

00:34:23.280 --> 00:34:26.100
that foundational idea
in balanced literacy,

00:34:26.100 --> 00:34:29.370
that letters are one way
to figure out a word.

00:34:29.370 --> 00:34:31.020
So kids are also taught
that when they come

00:34:31.020 --> 00:34:32.430
to a word they don't know,

00:34:32.430 --> 00:34:34.980
they can use pictures and context to try

00:34:34.980 --> 00:34:36.480
to come up with a guess.

00:34:36.480 --> 00:34:39.480
So Margaret started
teaching this LLI Program,

00:34:39.480 --> 00:34:40.830
and then at about the same time,

00:34:40.830 --> 00:34:43.470
she found this bunch of
unopened materials sitting

00:34:43.470 --> 00:34:44.910
on a shelf in her school,

00:34:44.910 --> 00:34:46.590
which you'll find all over America

00:34:46.590 --> 00:34:47.640
and is part of the problem.

00:34:47.640 --> 00:34:49.710
And it was a systematic phonics program

00:34:49.710 --> 00:34:52.230
and phonemic awareness and it teaches kids

00:34:52.230 --> 00:34:54.180
that when they come to
a word they don't know,

00:34:54.180 --> 00:34:56.790
they sound it out and
it does not include any

00:34:56.790 --> 00:34:58.110
of the cueing stuff.

00:34:58.110 --> 00:34:59.880
And in this program,

00:34:59.880 --> 00:35:03.420
beginning readers practice
reading in decodable books,

00:35:03.420 --> 00:35:05.850
books that contain words with
spelling patterns they've

00:35:05.850 --> 00:35:08.700
been taught so they don't
have to guess at the words,

00:35:08.700 --> 00:35:11.190
they don't have to use that strategy.

00:35:11.190 --> 00:35:13.050
So Margaret started
teaching some of her groups

00:35:13.050 --> 00:35:15.150
of students LLI with the cueing,

00:35:15.150 --> 00:35:17.280
and some, she taught
this systematic phonics

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:19.650
and phonemic awareness
program with no cueing.

00:35:19.650 --> 00:35:22.230
And, she started to notice differences

00:35:22.230 --> 00:35:24.210
between the two groups of kids,

00:35:24.210 --> 00:35:26.490
not just in how well they were reading,

00:35:26.490 --> 00:35:28.680
but in the way they
approached their reading.

00:35:28.680 --> 00:35:31.710
So she and a colleague
recorded first graders talking

00:35:31.710 --> 00:35:34.980
about what makes them good readers.

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:36.030
So this is a video,

00:35:36.030 --> 00:35:37.050
we're gonna play it for you.

00:35:37.050 --> 00:35:38.760
Mia is in the white shirt.

00:35:38.760 --> 00:35:40.890
She was learning phonics and no cueing.

00:35:40.890 --> 00:35:43.350
And Jabrea is in the pink jacket.

00:35:43.350 --> 00:35:44.970
She was taught the cueing system.

00:35:44.970 --> 00:35:45.960
So you can play the slide.

00:35:45.960 --> 00:35:48.450
- [Teacher] Yes, what
makes you good readers?

00:35:48.450 --> 00:35:49.663
- I learn a lot.

00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:54.670
- 'Cause I look at the
pictures and I read it.

00:35:54.670 --> 00:35:57.420
- [Teacher] Oh, do you
remember when you were little

00:35:57.420 --> 00:35:59.064
and you didn't know how to read?

00:35:59.064 --> 00:36:02.643
- Yes.

00:36:05.019 --> 00:36:06.030
- [Teacher] Like when you

00:36:06.030 --> 00:36:07.159
started kindergarten?
- Kind of.

00:36:07.159 --> 00:36:07.992
- Yeah.

00:36:09.060 --> 00:36:10.530
- [Teacher] What helped
you learn how to read?

00:36:10.530 --> 00:36:11.523
How did you learn?

00:36:13.800 --> 00:36:15.333
- By looking at the pictures.

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:17.153
- [Teacher] Anything else?

00:36:17.153 --> 00:36:20.347
- Looking at the words
and sounding them out.

00:36:22.440 --> 00:36:24.050
- So Margaret was seen
as over and over again

00:36:24.050 --> 00:36:25.260
in these two groups of kids.

00:36:25.260 --> 00:36:27.540
Like, one group of kids
was taking away this idea

00:36:27.540 --> 00:36:28.890
from reading instruction,

00:36:28.890 --> 00:36:31.110
the idea that reading is
about looking closely at words

00:36:31.110 --> 00:36:32.490
and sounding them out.

00:36:32.490 --> 00:36:33.960
And the other group of kids was learning

00:36:33.960 --> 00:36:35.370
that when you come to
a word you don't know,

00:36:35.370 --> 00:36:37.080
you don't have to look
carefully at the word

00:36:37.080 --> 00:36:38.220
and try to connect the spelling

00:36:38.220 --> 00:36:39.540
with the pronunciation and the meaning.

00:36:39.540 --> 00:36:41.487
Instead, you can look away from the word

00:36:41.487 --> 00:36:42.563
and you can look at the pictures,

00:36:42.563 --> 00:36:44.940
you can look at the other
words in the sentence,

00:36:44.940 --> 00:36:47.460
search around for clues to
help you identify the word.

00:36:47.460 --> 00:36:50.250
But remember, that orthographic
mapping process requires you

00:36:50.250 --> 00:36:51.540
to look carefully at the words

00:36:51.540 --> 00:36:53.730
so your brain links the spelling

00:36:53.730 --> 00:36:54.840
with the sounds and the meaning.

00:36:54.840 --> 00:36:57.300
And this cueing stuff teaches kids

00:36:57.300 --> 00:36:59.550
to actually look away from the words.

00:36:59.550 --> 00:37:01.830
So what Margaret Goldberg
said to me about the kids

00:37:01.830 --> 00:37:04.297
in her LLI groups was this,

00:37:04.297 --> 00:37:07.147
"I did lasting damage to these kids.

00:37:07.147 --> 00:37:08.917
"It was so hard to ever get them

00:37:08.917 --> 00:37:12.907
"to stop looking at a picture
to guess what a word would be.

00:37:12.907 --> 00:37:15.607
"It was so hard to ever
get them to slow down

00:37:15.607 --> 00:37:17.677
"and sound a word out because they had

00:37:17.677 --> 00:37:20.527
"had this experience of
knowing that you predict

00:37:20.527 --> 00:37:23.880
"what you read before you read it."

00:37:23.880 --> 00:37:25.470
So you can go to the next slide.

00:37:25.470 --> 00:37:28.140
This sort of tease up the
"Sold a Story" podcast,

00:37:28.140 --> 00:37:33.140
which is really my
effort to figure out why,

00:37:33.390 --> 00:37:37.020
how did it happen that this
idea became so predominant

00:37:37.020 --> 00:37:38.730
in American schools,

00:37:38.730 --> 00:37:40.220
not just in American
schools but in schools

00:37:40.220 --> 00:37:42.480
in other parts of the
English-speaking world too?

00:37:42.480 --> 00:37:43.313
Like I said,

00:37:43.313 --> 00:37:46.380
we had this unbelievable
timeline that started in 1649.

00:37:46.380 --> 00:37:50.490
It has 2019 entries as of yesterday,

00:37:50.490 --> 00:37:53.310
and it is about one idea.

00:37:53.310 --> 00:37:56.580
The idea is kids don't need
to sound out written words

00:37:56.580 --> 00:37:59.040
because they have other
ways to know what they are.

00:37:59.040 --> 00:38:01.680
They can sound out the words
but they don't have to.

00:38:01.680 --> 00:38:05.310
The whole thing is about
what's wrong with that idea

00:38:05.310 --> 00:38:08.400
and how that idea came to
dominate in American schools

00:38:08.400 --> 00:38:10.500
and the damage that idea has done.

00:38:10.500 --> 00:38:12.300
Six episodes, four and a half hours,

00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:13.770
that's it.

00:38:13.770 --> 00:38:15.270
The debate about how to teach children

00:38:15.270 --> 00:38:17.400
to read has long been about phonics

00:38:17.400 --> 00:38:19.620
and has been understood
to be about phonics.

00:38:19.620 --> 00:38:22.290
And getting reading instruction
right though is not just

00:38:22.290 --> 00:38:24.540
about adding phonics instruction,

00:38:24.540 --> 00:38:27.930
it's about taking away that cueing theory.

00:38:27.930 --> 00:38:29.400
And I think most importantly,

00:38:29.400 --> 00:38:32.730
helping teachers understand
what's wrong with that theory.

00:38:32.730 --> 00:38:35.130
And I think you do that by
helping them learn how to read,

00:38:35.130 --> 00:38:37.110
and when teachers really do learn

00:38:37.110 --> 00:38:39.090
how kids learn how to read,

00:38:39.090 --> 00:38:42.630
then they sit there and it
sort of falls apart on its own.

00:38:42.630 --> 00:38:44.370
This whole cueing theory,

00:38:44.370 --> 00:38:45.390
they're like, what,

00:38:45.390 --> 00:38:47.610
why did I do that?

00:38:47.610 --> 00:38:48.930
And we can get into a little bit

00:38:48.930 --> 00:38:50.130
of that in the conversation.

00:38:50.130 --> 00:38:52.050
But I'll just tell you a few
things about the reaction

00:38:52.050 --> 00:38:52.883
to "Sold a Story".

00:38:52.883 --> 00:38:54.990
It's had lots of listeners.

00:38:54.990 --> 00:38:58.200
I think it's 5.3 million listeners so far,

00:38:58.200 --> 00:38:59.970
lots of people who aren't educators,

00:38:59.970 --> 00:39:01.293
who aren't the parents
of struggling readers.

00:39:01.293 --> 00:39:02.850
That was really one of my goals.

00:39:02.850 --> 00:39:04.410
I said to my editor early on,

00:39:04.410 --> 00:39:08.820
I really want the moms of the
kids who aren't struggling

00:39:08.820 --> 00:39:09.870
to write to me and say,

00:39:09.870 --> 00:39:11.220
I listened and I care about this

00:39:11.220 --> 00:39:12.390
and I wanna do something about it.

00:39:12.390 --> 00:39:13.410
And that is what's happening.

00:39:13.410 --> 00:39:15.720
I think the parents of struggling readers

00:39:15.720 --> 00:39:17.850
have felt alone for a long time,

00:39:17.850 --> 00:39:20.070
and they're finding each
other and there's more

00:39:20.070 --> 00:39:21.210
of them than you realize.

00:39:21.210 --> 00:39:22.350
And I think this is something

00:39:22.350 --> 00:39:23.850
that all of us need to care about,

00:39:23.850 --> 00:39:25.890
even people who learned how to read easily

00:39:25.890 --> 00:39:28.740
in spite of how they were taught.

00:39:28.740 --> 00:39:30.990
Another thing that "Sold
a Story" has done is

00:39:30.990 --> 00:39:33.420
that legislators in at least 15 states

00:39:33.420 --> 00:39:35.430
have introduced some kind of bill

00:39:35.430 --> 00:39:37.920
to overhaul reading
instruction since it came out.

00:39:37.920 --> 00:39:40.350
There was a big wave of
legislative effort going

00:39:40.350 --> 00:39:41.340
on before that.

00:39:41.340 --> 00:39:42.720
So this has really been happening

00:39:42.720 --> 00:39:44.010
over the past several years,

00:39:44.010 --> 00:39:47.280
and I think a lot of it has
to do with parent advocacy.

00:39:47.280 --> 00:39:50.760
But one of the things that's
happening now in these bills is

00:39:50.760 --> 00:39:52.860
that a lot of them are
focused on curriculum,

00:39:52.860 --> 00:39:54.600
and some of them are specifically focused

00:39:54.600 --> 00:39:57.510
on banning curriculum that
includes this cueing idea.

00:39:57.510 --> 00:39:58.980
At last count as of yesterday,

00:39:58.980 --> 00:40:00.330
there were 10 states at least

00:40:00.330 --> 00:40:02.610
that have introduced
some kind of cueing ban,

00:40:02.610 --> 00:40:04.050
which I have some mixed feelings about

00:40:04.050 --> 00:40:05.310
which we can talk about.

00:40:05.310 --> 00:40:07.290
I mean, the point of this podcast was

00:40:07.290 --> 00:40:09.630
that this idea is debunked and harmful

00:40:09.630 --> 00:40:11.370
and breaking its decades-long hold

00:40:11.370 --> 00:40:14.490
on education system is really important.

00:40:14.490 --> 00:40:15.873
But these bands make me anxious.

00:40:15.873 --> 00:40:17.100
When you take something away,

00:40:17.100 --> 00:40:18.810
everyone's suddenly
looking for something new.

00:40:18.810 --> 00:40:19.890
I'm worried about,

00:40:19.890 --> 00:40:22.470
there's sort of a rush to buy new things.

00:40:22.470 --> 00:40:24.360
I don't think buying
something new is the answer.

00:40:24.360 --> 00:40:26.370
I don't think a new
curriculum's gonna solve

00:40:26.370 --> 00:40:27.203
all the problems,

00:40:27.203 --> 00:40:28.036
'cause at the end of the day,

00:40:28.036 --> 00:40:29.670
this is really about teacher knowledge.

00:40:29.670 --> 00:40:30.780
Teachers teach kids.

00:40:30.780 --> 00:40:32.400
Curriculum don't teach kids.

00:40:32.400 --> 00:40:34.860
And banning this cueing
stuff is taking place

00:40:34.860 --> 00:40:36.720
at a time when lots of other
books are being banned,

00:40:36.720 --> 00:40:38.160
so I think there's a danger here

00:40:38.160 --> 00:40:40.260
that this could all get caught up

00:40:40.260 --> 00:40:41.940
and corrupted by partisan politics.

00:40:41.940 --> 00:40:44.760
And we tried this once before in America

00:40:44.760 --> 00:40:46.230
and it did get caught up and, I think,

00:40:46.230 --> 00:40:48.570
sort of killed bipartisan politics.

00:40:48.570 --> 00:40:51.870
So I think I'm going to skip the rest

00:40:51.870 --> 00:40:52.770
of the stuff I was gonna say,

00:40:52.770 --> 00:40:54.000
'cause I think I've talked too long

00:40:54.000 --> 00:40:55.560
and I would really love
to get to your questions.

00:40:55.560 --> 00:40:57.330
So I'll just wrap up there

00:40:57.330 --> 00:41:00.033
and I'm looking forward
to this discussion.

00:41:01.020 --> 00:41:03.453
- Okay, thank you so much Emily.

00:41:04.530 --> 00:41:05.363
That was was great.

00:41:05.363 --> 00:41:06.993
And I wanna say also,

00:41:08.250 --> 00:41:12.030
the podcast, it's super educational

00:41:12.030 --> 00:41:14.220
and also really interesting

00:41:14.220 --> 00:41:18.420
and I think you really
breathe a tremendous amount

00:41:18.420 --> 00:41:23.420
of life into the science
of reading and phonics

00:41:23.899 --> 00:41:27.477
and orthographic mappings
and things like that.

00:41:27.477 --> 00:41:30.790
I'm not surprised that millions of people

00:41:32.490 --> 00:41:35.730
have listened to your podcasts

00:41:35.730 --> 00:41:38.040
and that it's had the
impact that it's had.

00:41:38.040 --> 00:41:42.180
I wanna remind our participants,

00:41:42.180 --> 00:41:44.836
please ask questions.

00:41:44.836 --> 00:41:47.047
"When will you get the opportunity

00:41:47.047 --> 00:41:50.580
"to ask Emily Hanford a question?"

00:41:50.580 --> 00:41:53.763
Well, now is when you'll
have that opportunity.

00:41:54.960 --> 00:41:56.580
So please send your questions

00:41:56.580 --> 00:42:00.990
and then Eric Patashnik
is gonna filter those

00:42:00.990 --> 00:42:05.190
and turn them into maybe
groups of questions

00:42:05.190 --> 00:42:07.650
and things like that for Emily.

00:42:07.650 --> 00:42:10.110
But let me ask Emily a question,

00:42:10.110 --> 00:42:14.309
following up on what you said at the end

00:42:14.309 --> 00:42:17.013
of your presentation about the politics.

00:42:18.930 --> 00:42:20.670
As you mentioned,

00:42:20.670 --> 00:42:23.470
there's this big wave
of legislation right now

00:42:24.678 --> 00:42:25.590
and it's doing,

00:42:25.590 --> 00:42:26.423
as you said,

00:42:26.423 --> 00:42:27.256
all sorts of things.

00:42:27.256 --> 00:42:28.230
It's promoting phonics.

00:42:28.230 --> 00:42:29.850
It's, in some cases I guess,

00:42:29.850 --> 00:42:32.403
restricting the use of cueing.

00:42:32.403 --> 00:42:36.783
It's fairly detailed I
would say, for legislation.

00:42:37.680 --> 00:42:40.015
We've had a moment in the United States,

00:42:40.015 --> 00:42:42.480
the No Child Left Behind experience,

00:42:42.480 --> 00:42:45.090
I think, which is what
you were alluding to.

00:42:45.090 --> 00:42:47.460
In 2002,

00:42:47.460 --> 00:42:51.180
a bill passed under George Bush,

00:42:51.180 --> 00:42:52.893
but it was a bipartisan bill.

00:42:54.065 --> 00:42:58.097
I think it had like almost
90 votes in the US Senate,

00:42:59.520 --> 00:43:03.390
almost maybe 380 or something
like that in the House.

00:43:03.390 --> 00:43:05.250
- Unheard of in today's times,

00:43:05.250 --> 00:43:06.180
yes.
- Yeah, right.

00:43:06.180 --> 00:43:07.830
So big bipartisan thing.

00:43:07.830 --> 00:43:08.663
When I think about it,

00:43:08.663 --> 00:43:10.830
I think I may be misremembering,

00:43:10.830 --> 00:43:15.330
but I think I even remember
Ted Kennedy standing next

00:43:15.330 --> 00:43:18.493
to George Bush when they signed this bill.

00:43:18.493 --> 00:43:21.210
- Definitely, it has
been photographed yes.

00:43:21.210 --> 00:43:22.470
- Good, good. Okay.

00:43:22.470 --> 00:43:26.010
And then the Reading First
Program that came out

00:43:26.010 --> 00:43:29.440
of the No Child Left Behind Bill

00:43:30.390 --> 00:43:32.250
was sort of aimed at the idea

00:43:32.250 --> 00:43:36.933
of promoting scientifically-based
reading approaches.

00:43:37.830 --> 00:43:40.227
And so this had a real head of steam.

00:43:40.227 --> 00:43:43.230
This was based on a national assessment

00:43:43.230 --> 00:43:46.080
of where the science of reading was.

00:43:46.080 --> 00:43:49.950
And it was very far along and very close

00:43:49.950 --> 00:43:52.143
to what you are describing.

00:43:53.040 --> 00:43:54.100
This effort

00:43:56.100 --> 00:43:57.510
kind of ran off the rails,

00:43:57.510 --> 00:43:58.439
I would say.

00:43:58.439 --> 00:44:00.750
Within half a decade or so,

00:44:00.750 --> 00:44:02.400
it began to run off the rails,

00:44:02.400 --> 00:44:03.513
ran off the rails.

00:44:04.980 --> 00:44:09.980
What lessons do you draw
from that experience?

00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:11.500
What cautions

00:44:13.350 --> 00:44:17.040
are you led to wanna kind of remark on?

00:44:17.040 --> 00:44:21.939
What is our takeaway from that lesson?

00:44:21.939 --> 00:44:24.780
What's your takeaway from that experience,

00:44:24.780 --> 00:44:28.710
to help us avoid getting into a kind of,

00:44:28.710 --> 00:44:30.900
turning what really is something

00:44:30.900 --> 00:44:34.620
that I think everyone
could really get behind

00:44:34.620 --> 00:44:38.010
as the initial coalition behind

00:44:38.010 --> 00:44:42.981
the No Child Left Behind
Acts suggests that,

00:44:42.981 --> 00:44:45.040
what could turn this into a

00:44:46.698 --> 00:44:51.698
partisan thing and lead to
some of the promise that we see

00:44:51.780 --> 00:44:54.870
in this initial legislative way

00:44:54.870 --> 00:44:58.170
of sort of not being realized?

00:44:58.170 --> 00:45:01.980
- Yeah, well certainly for
a lot of people out there,

00:45:01.980 --> 00:45:03.864
it's a little bit deja vu all over again.

00:45:03.864 --> 00:45:04.980
What's happening now,

00:45:04.980 --> 00:45:05.813
they're like, wait a minute,

00:45:05.813 --> 00:45:07.443
didn't we do this before?

00:45:09.180 --> 00:45:10.440
And I'll say a couple of things.

00:45:10.440 --> 00:45:14.370
Number one, I'm convinced from
all the work that I've done

00:45:14.370 --> 00:45:16.620
that what's happening
now wouldn't be happening

00:45:16.620 --> 00:45:18.690
if reading Reading First hadn't happened,

00:45:18.690 --> 00:45:20.700
in both a positive and a negative way.

00:45:20.700 --> 00:45:23.100
So, one thing that I think is true,

00:45:23.100 --> 00:45:26.580
so Reading First was a
basically $6 billion effort.

00:45:26.580 --> 00:45:27.540
It was about a billion,

00:45:27.540 --> 00:45:31.170
so we taxpayers invested
a lot of money in trying

00:45:31.170 --> 00:45:33.240
to get the so-called science
of reading into schools.

00:45:33.240 --> 00:45:36.346
It's really very similar
to what's happening today,

00:45:36.346 --> 00:45:39.567
and essentially, it was
motivated by scientists

00:45:40.410 --> 00:45:42.060
and policymakers recognizing

00:45:42.060 --> 00:45:44.250
that there was this big gap
between research and practice,

00:45:44.250 --> 00:45:45.420
and we knew a lot from research

00:45:45.420 --> 00:45:47.010
and we just needed to
get this into schools.

00:45:47.010 --> 00:45:48.390
And if we could just get this into schools

00:45:48.390 --> 00:45:50.610
and get the right materials
into teacher's hands,

00:45:50.610 --> 00:45:52.050
we could make a big difference.

00:45:52.050 --> 00:45:56.610
I think what's happening
now is benefiting from that

00:45:56.610 --> 00:45:58.770
because in every school
and every school system,

00:45:58.770 --> 00:46:00.480
there are some people

00:46:00.480 --> 00:46:03.270
who really's lives were
changed by Reading First.

00:46:03.270 --> 00:46:05.190
They really did learn something about

00:46:05.190 --> 00:46:06.600
how kids learn to read,

00:46:06.600 --> 00:46:09.330
and they've been trying
their hardest to do that

00:46:09.330 --> 00:46:11.160
or there's a base of knowledge there.

00:46:11.160 --> 00:46:13.620
So there is within our
system where there's a huge,

00:46:13.620 --> 00:46:15.420
there's a lot of turnover and all that.

00:46:15.420 --> 00:46:16.800
There's still a base of knowledge

00:46:16.800 --> 00:46:18.810
from a generation ago now practically.

00:46:18.810 --> 00:46:21.690
And so, I don't think what's happening now

00:46:21.690 --> 00:46:23.670
would be happening if we
hadn't sort of established

00:46:23.670 --> 00:46:26.100
that baseline and all the research

00:46:26.100 --> 00:46:28.503
that was done at that point.

00:46:29.400 --> 00:46:31.110
The other thing that
happened I really think is

00:46:31.110 --> 00:46:33.240
that we had a reaction to Reading First.

00:46:33.240 --> 00:46:35.970
So a lot of what's happened
in the years since,

00:46:35.970 --> 00:46:38.670
I think we had a decade of
really swinging really far

00:46:38.670 --> 00:46:40.230
in a different direction.

00:46:40.230 --> 00:46:42.150
There were a number of things
that people didn't like

00:46:42.150 --> 00:46:44.130
about the No Child Left Behind Bill.

00:46:44.130 --> 00:46:45.630
There were a number of things in there

00:46:45.630 --> 00:46:46.560
that I think even the people

00:46:46.560 --> 00:46:48.930
who made the bill recognized
were mistakes, right.

00:46:48.930 --> 00:46:51.420
So I think that there was a
lot of reaction against that.

00:46:51.420 --> 00:46:53.010
There was a lot of political reaction

00:46:53.010 --> 00:46:55.470
against the Bush
administration, the Republicans.

00:46:55.470 --> 00:46:57.090
We went from a Republican administration

00:46:57.090 --> 00:46:58.950
to a Democratic administration.

00:46:58.950 --> 00:47:01.000
There were a lot of things going on here.

00:47:02.317 --> 00:47:05.375
I think mistakes were
made during Reading First.

00:47:05.375 --> 00:47:08.190
I think a lot of it was
very top-down in nature

00:47:08.190 --> 00:47:10.350
and I think that's a very
important lesson now,

00:47:10.350 --> 00:47:13.080
and I think that's why we're
at a fragile moment right now.

00:47:13.080 --> 00:47:14.940
'Cause I think a lot of the conversation

00:47:14.940 --> 00:47:17.790
about the science of reading
right now has been coming

00:47:17.790 --> 00:47:19.140
from the bottom-up.

00:47:19.140 --> 00:47:21.720
There's been a lot of parent advocacy,

00:47:21.720 --> 00:47:23.640
teachers too, saying,

00:47:23.640 --> 00:47:25.590
hey, we have a problem here.

00:47:25.590 --> 00:47:27.240
We need to fix this problem.

00:47:27.240 --> 00:47:29.010
And they've been sort of
looking up and saying,

00:47:29.010 --> 00:47:30.750
we need help from our principals,

00:47:30.750 --> 00:47:31.920
from our superintendents,

00:47:31.920 --> 00:47:33.960
from our state legislators.

00:47:33.960 --> 00:47:38.070
And in some cases they were responding,

00:47:38.070 --> 00:47:39.630
but in other cases they're saying, like,

00:47:39.630 --> 00:47:40.463
hey, we need something,

00:47:40.463 --> 00:47:41.296
we need help here.

00:47:41.296 --> 00:47:42.129
And now that's happening.

00:47:42.129 --> 00:47:45.600
So now we're starting to get
a very much of a top-down.

00:47:45.600 --> 00:47:46.800
I think good policy,

00:47:46.800 --> 00:47:48.660
and I think for the political
scientists on the call,

00:47:48.660 --> 00:47:50.640
I think ultimately good policy comes

00:47:50.640 --> 00:47:53.100
from some really delicate
balance between these two things.

00:47:53.100 --> 00:47:56.278
So I think we're in the
midst of that right now.

00:47:56.278 --> 00:47:58.050
But I think it's important, I mean,

00:47:58.050 --> 00:48:00.360
I think there were some
things that were heavy-handed

00:48:00.360 --> 00:48:02.160
and too top-down about Reading First,

00:48:02.160 --> 00:48:04.920
so I think we need to be
careful about top-down stuff.

00:48:04.920 --> 00:48:08.403
But again, top-down is because
people below need the help.

00:48:09.480 --> 00:48:11.643
I think that Reading First,

00:48:12.540 --> 00:48:14.550
the story about Reading First,

00:48:14.550 --> 00:48:16.830
and I think this actually
happened in practice

00:48:16.830 --> 00:48:17.663
in a lot of places,

00:48:17.663 --> 00:48:21.360
is that it sort of focused
too much on the decoding part.

00:48:21.360 --> 00:48:22.920
One of the reasons I decided to give you

00:48:22.920 --> 00:48:24.660
that whole explanation
about how kids learn

00:48:24.660 --> 00:48:26.100
to read is I want you to understand

00:48:26.100 --> 00:48:28.710
that this is not just
about phonics, right.

00:48:28.710 --> 00:48:32.310
That language comprehension part is huge.

00:48:32.310 --> 00:48:34.770
I want people to understand
that you can't just add phonics

00:48:34.770 --> 00:48:36.060
'cause that's not enough.

00:48:36.060 --> 00:48:38.430
And you can't just add
phonics without taking

00:48:38.430 --> 00:48:40.410
away the stuff that isn't right

00:48:40.410 --> 00:48:41.970
about the whole language theory.

00:48:41.970 --> 00:48:44.970
So I think in Reading
First time went large,

00:48:44.970 --> 00:48:47.070
we probably just didn't
do enough of the work

00:48:47.070 --> 00:48:48.570
on the language comprehension

00:48:48.570 --> 00:48:50.970
and the cueing stuff didn't go away.

00:48:50.970 --> 00:48:55.970
We didn't actually lose the
adherence to the basic beliefs

00:48:56.460 --> 00:48:58.350
in the whole language approach.

00:48:58.350 --> 00:49:00.480
And I think maybe now,

00:49:00.480 --> 00:49:04.110
maybe the conversation about this

00:49:04.110 --> 00:49:05.850
and the research itself is sort

00:49:05.850 --> 00:49:09.300
of becoming more nuanced
and sophisticated,

00:49:09.300 --> 00:49:12.840
that I'm hopeful that we
might be at a good moment.

00:49:12.840 --> 00:49:15.090
And I'll say one last
thing about the politics.

00:49:15.090 --> 00:49:19.080
I do think that Reading
First became associated with,

00:49:19.080 --> 00:49:20.370
because it was a George Bush,

00:49:20.370 --> 00:49:21.480
you know, it was a Republican

00:49:21.480 --> 00:49:23.730
and phonics has always been
seen as sort of traditional

00:49:23.730 --> 00:49:26.070
and conservative and old-fashioned.

00:49:26.070 --> 00:49:28.410
And as soon as that got
associated with phonics

00:49:28.410 --> 00:49:29.790
and the George Bush administration,

00:49:29.790 --> 00:49:30.990
it was easy to dismiss.

00:49:30.990 --> 00:49:31.830
If you were progressive,

00:49:31.830 --> 00:49:33.510
if you were liberal or whatever,

00:49:33.510 --> 00:49:35.940
it was like push that away.

00:49:35.940 --> 00:49:39.540
What I'm hoping is gonna mediate it now is

00:49:39.540 --> 00:49:43.590
that this is really
important to a lot of people

00:49:43.590 --> 00:49:44.970
who have a very,

00:49:44.970 --> 00:49:46.710
very wide set of political beliefs.

00:49:46.710 --> 00:49:48.390
So if you just look at my Twitter feed,

00:49:48.390 --> 00:49:51.300
it's got people on the far left
and people on the far right

00:49:51.300 --> 00:49:52.590
and everywhere in between,

00:49:52.590 --> 00:49:54.540
and I love that.
(Emily chuckling)

00:49:54.540 --> 00:49:57.420
I love that there is that
wide swathe of people

00:49:57.420 --> 00:49:59.220
who are really interested in this.

00:49:59.220 --> 00:50:02.790
So I hope that what will happen
here is that if it starts

00:50:02.790 --> 00:50:06.450
to get taken by one party or another,

00:50:06.450 --> 00:50:08.460
that there'll be a force being like, no,

00:50:08.460 --> 00:50:09.540
no, no, no, no, no.

00:50:09.540 --> 00:50:12.030
If you overhear on the
far right wanna take this

00:50:12.030 --> 00:50:12.863
as your issue,

00:50:12.863 --> 00:50:13.800
I'm not gonna let you do that.

00:50:13.800 --> 00:50:17.610
I am over here on the far left
and this issue is mine too.

00:50:17.610 --> 00:50:19.830
Same thing, someone gets
very far left about this,

00:50:19.830 --> 00:50:20.663
no, no, no, no,

00:50:20.663 --> 00:50:22.350
this is my issue too.

00:50:22.350 --> 00:50:24.000
I'm hoping that that's
what's gonna happen,

00:50:24.000 --> 00:50:26.760
but it's possible I'm
being too optimistic.

00:50:26.760 --> 00:50:28.320
- Great, well thanks again.

00:50:28.320 --> 00:50:30.540
Thank you for that response, Emily,

00:50:30.540 --> 00:50:34.673
and again, thank you for
such an engaging talk.

00:50:34.673 --> 00:50:37.830
We have a bunch of questions, and again,

00:50:37.830 --> 00:50:39.457
I wanna encourage people,

00:50:39.457 --> 00:50:42.120
we have a bunch of questions
and I hope we'll get

00:50:42.120 --> 00:50:43.740
to a bunch more.

00:50:43.740 --> 00:50:46.530
So please ask your questions?

00:50:46.530 --> 00:50:50.250
With that, I wanna turn
it over to Eric Patashnik,

00:50:50.250 --> 00:50:54.003
who has been processing the question flow.

00:50:55.200 --> 00:50:57.000
- Thank you, it's a
real pleasure to be here

00:50:57.000 --> 00:51:00.420
and we've received lots
of comments from people

00:51:00.420 --> 00:51:03.390
who said that Emily's podcast was amazing,

00:51:03.390 --> 00:51:04.920
and I certainly will echo that.

00:51:04.920 --> 00:51:06.960
It is a phenomenal
experience to listen to it

00:51:06.960 --> 00:51:08.940
and so encourage everyone to do that.

00:51:08.940 --> 00:51:11.032
So, one of the questions,

00:51:11.032 --> 00:51:13.950
Emily, was about your podcast

00:51:13.950 --> 00:51:15.990
and where Lucy Caulkins referenced

00:51:15.990 --> 00:51:19.200
classroom literacy practices
in the '70s and '80s

00:51:19.200 --> 00:51:22.350
as the antithesis of
exciting and beautiful.

00:51:22.350 --> 00:51:24.237
And so the question is,

00:51:24.237 --> 00:51:25.747
"Is one of the reasons

00:51:25.747 --> 00:51:28.687
"why science of reading
wasn't getting much traction

00:51:28.687 --> 00:51:30.367
"was its image problem,

00:51:30.367 --> 00:51:31.957
"that there was an impression

00:51:31.957 --> 00:51:35.407
"that classroom practices
associated with it

00:51:35.407 --> 00:51:38.557
"are kind of boring and are
not gonna stimulate children?

00:51:38.557 --> 00:51:41.197
"How important do you
think that dimension was

00:51:41.197 --> 00:51:44.797
"to why it didn't gain more traction

00:51:44.797 --> 00:51:46.980
"in schools around the country?"

00:51:46.980 --> 00:51:49.500
- Yes, I think that is a good observation

00:51:49.500 --> 00:51:51.120
and I think that is definitely part of,

00:51:51.120 --> 00:51:52.920
I think, that the science of reading did

00:51:52.920 --> 00:51:56.070
and maybe still does have a
little bit of an image problem.

00:51:56.070 --> 00:51:58.530
I will also say that I
think it's really important

00:51:58.530 --> 00:52:00.510
when people are talking
about something that's boring

00:52:00.510 --> 00:52:02.670
to think about who is it boring for?

00:52:02.670 --> 00:52:06.000
I think in some cases this
stuff is boring for the adults,

00:52:06.000 --> 00:52:07.980
but not necessarily
boring for the children.

00:52:07.980 --> 00:52:10.560
If you are in a classroom
where kids are really learning

00:52:10.560 --> 00:52:12.960
how to read and cracking the code,

00:52:12.960 --> 00:52:14.910
they're pretty excited about that.

00:52:14.910 --> 00:52:16.650
And I think some of us can even remember,

00:52:16.650 --> 00:52:18.720
I know people can remember those moments

00:52:18.720 --> 00:52:21.450
where it clicked for them where
they were finally reading.

00:52:21.450 --> 00:52:24.480
It's an incredibly
powerful and exciting thing

00:52:24.480 --> 00:52:27.000
for a child to begin to learn how to read.

00:52:27.000 --> 00:52:30.210
It's really just like a big life moment.

00:52:30.210 --> 00:52:32.610
And I think that a lot of the,

00:52:32.610 --> 00:52:35.250
what we know is that
there's a significant amount

00:52:35.250 --> 00:52:38.520
of just details that
kids need to understand

00:52:38.520 --> 00:52:41.490
in repetition and practice
that can look boring

00:52:41.490 --> 00:52:43.020
and rote from the outside,

00:52:43.020 --> 00:52:44.730
but if it's effective,

00:52:44.730 --> 00:52:47.160
if it is getting kids to
where they need to be,

00:52:47.160 --> 00:52:48.750
remember the question I kept asking,

00:52:48.750 --> 00:52:51.270
how does a little kid get there?

00:52:51.270 --> 00:52:52.470
And the question is,

00:52:52.470 --> 00:52:53.970
you're not gonna love reading,

00:52:53.970 --> 00:52:55.230
it's not gonna be a joyful,

00:52:55.230 --> 00:52:57.600
beautiful experience if you can't do it.

00:52:57.600 --> 00:52:58.980
And it is not a joyful,

00:52:58.980 --> 00:53:01.440
beautiful experience for
a lot of kids in a lot

00:53:01.440 --> 00:53:04.110
of classrooms where they're
really being expected

00:53:04.110 --> 00:53:06.090
to figure out a lot of
this stuff on their own.

00:53:06.090 --> 00:53:08.550
They're being given sort of big,

00:53:08.550 --> 00:53:10.770
broad instruction and some strategies

00:53:10.770 --> 00:53:11.927
to figure out the words,

00:53:11.927 --> 00:53:15.000
and it all adds up for some kids,

00:53:15.000 --> 00:53:17.370
but for a lot of kids it doesn't add up.

00:53:17.370 --> 00:53:19.710
And again, I wanna go back
to the equity issue here.

00:53:19.710 --> 00:53:21.490
There are some kids

00:53:22.380 --> 00:53:24.900
who get the help they
need outside of school,

00:53:24.900 --> 00:53:26.580
but there are a lot of kids who don't.

00:53:26.580 --> 00:53:30.540
And so we're really I think dramatically

00:53:30.540 --> 00:53:32.280
widening

00:53:32.280 --> 00:53:33.810
gaps,

00:53:33.810 --> 00:53:37.470
opportunity gaps, very
early on in education

00:53:37.470 --> 00:53:40.680
when we don't teach kids directly

00:53:40.680 --> 00:53:43.467
and explicitly the things we want them

00:53:43.467 --> 00:53:44.973
and need them to know.

00:53:47.070 --> 00:53:48.690
- Great, another question,

00:53:48.690 --> 00:53:51.277
here's a two-parter and that is,

00:53:51.277 --> 00:53:54.097
"Are there particular
phonics or decoding programs

00:53:54.097 --> 00:53:57.697
"that the experts who study the
science of reading advocate,

00:53:57.697 --> 00:53:59.137
"that really identify and say,

00:53:59.137 --> 00:54:01.387
"these are the best practices?

00:54:01.387 --> 00:54:02.377
"And then, secondly,

00:54:02.377 --> 00:54:04.627
"what would be the advice you would offer

00:54:04.627 --> 00:54:07.057
"to somebody who finds that the schools,

00:54:07.057 --> 00:54:08.887
"perhaps the schools
their children are in,

00:54:08.887 --> 00:54:12.877
"is resistant to moving away
from the cueing curriculum?

00:54:12.877 --> 00:54:15.097
"What kinds of advocacy
strategies are you finding

00:54:15.097 --> 00:54:17.837
"are most effective on the ground?"

00:54:17.837 --> 00:54:18.900
- So the first question

00:54:18.900 --> 00:54:20.760
about programs I get all the time

00:54:20.760 --> 00:54:23.370
and I'm reluctant to answer that.

00:54:23.370 --> 00:54:24.870
Number one, I'm not hiding anything.

00:54:24.870 --> 00:54:26.880
I don't really even know
if I know the answer

00:54:26.880 --> 00:54:27.750
to that, right.

00:54:27.750 --> 00:54:29.680
But I think people's immediate

00:54:30.833 --> 00:54:32.700
question is, well what do I buy?

00:54:32.700 --> 00:54:33.600
What do I need to get?

00:54:33.600 --> 00:54:36.120
And I always wanna tell everyone, hold on,

00:54:36.120 --> 00:54:38.658
let's make sure you really understand

00:54:38.658 --> 00:54:40.650
what you're looking for.

00:54:40.650 --> 00:54:43.800
So I do think there are some
places that really do lay out

00:54:43.800 --> 00:54:47.220
what are the kinds of things
you wanna see in a curriculum.

00:54:47.220 --> 00:54:48.210
And I think those are important.

00:54:48.210 --> 00:54:50.370
There's an organization
called the Reading League

00:54:50.370 --> 00:54:52.020
and they do have like some rubrics

00:54:52.020 --> 00:54:55.590
where you can go through
and evaluate curriculum.

00:54:55.590 --> 00:54:57.930
There are some entities that
actually evaluate curriculum.

00:54:57.930 --> 00:54:59.970
There are some states that
are starting to do that.

00:54:59.970 --> 00:55:01.980
I mean, Massachusetts is nearby.

00:55:01.980 --> 00:55:03.510
They're starting to have their own system

00:55:03.510 --> 00:55:04.530
where it's called, CURATE,

00:55:04.530 --> 00:55:07.470
where they're going through
and looking at curriculum.

00:55:07.470 --> 00:55:08.910
And I think they're really looking at it

00:55:08.910 --> 00:55:11.190
with the sort of science
of reading in mind.

00:55:11.190 --> 00:55:13.500
There are some other
organizations that do it

00:55:13.500 --> 00:55:15.750
that more look at whether
or not they kind of align

00:55:15.750 --> 00:55:17.160
with the common core standards,

00:55:17.160 --> 00:55:19.620
which isn't exactly the same as looking at

00:55:19.620 --> 00:55:21.540
whether they really align
with the science of reading,

00:55:21.540 --> 00:55:24.360
but EdReports is an
organization that does that.

00:55:24.360 --> 00:55:26.530
So I think it's important to

00:55:28.200 --> 00:55:31.347
just know what questions
to ask of a curriculum.

00:55:31.347 --> 00:55:33.450
So, I know that's maybe
an unsatisfying answer,

00:55:33.450 --> 00:55:34.350
but also, as a journalist,

00:55:34.350 --> 00:55:36.903
I never wanna endorse products anyway.

00:55:37.830 --> 00:55:38.663
On the question,

00:55:38.663 --> 00:55:41.633
I hear this all the time from
parents who are concerned

00:55:41.633 --> 00:55:44.640
and are getting resistance
from their schools.

00:55:44.640 --> 00:55:46.560
And I would say,

00:55:46.560 --> 00:55:48.540
find the other parents who
have the same questions

00:55:48.540 --> 00:55:50.787
and concerns you have
because they're there.

00:55:50.787 --> 00:55:52.590
And so, find them,

00:55:52.590 --> 00:55:54.900
and it's so much better
to go to the school

00:55:54.900 --> 00:55:56.253
with more than one of you.

00:55:57.630 --> 00:56:00.000
I also think many parents have gone

00:56:00.000 --> 00:56:01.590
to the school with these questions,

00:56:01.590 --> 00:56:03.840
and it's just them sort
of against the school.

00:56:03.840 --> 00:56:05.460
I mean, they literally walk into a meeting

00:56:05.460 --> 00:56:07.680
and there's like eight people, and them.

00:56:07.680 --> 00:56:09.670
And they get sort of this

00:56:11.100 --> 00:56:13.470
eduspeak that they don't
understand and then they leave,

00:56:13.470 --> 00:56:15.120
feeling like they're in
an alternate reality.

00:56:15.120 --> 00:56:15.953
And it's like,

00:56:15.953 --> 00:56:17.460
I think my kid is having trouble reading,

00:56:17.460 --> 00:56:18.293
but they're telling me all this stuff

00:56:18.293 --> 00:56:19.470
and I understand what they're saying

00:56:19.470 --> 00:56:21.120
and they're saying it's not a problem,

00:56:21.120 --> 00:56:22.410
and I know that it is.

00:56:22.410 --> 00:56:25.800
So find the other parents who are trying

00:56:25.800 --> 00:56:27.360
to ask questions and go with them.

00:56:27.360 --> 00:56:29.160
And I guess take advantage of the moment

00:56:29.160 --> 00:56:31.890
because I think there is a
growing awareness of this.

00:56:31.890 --> 00:56:34.320
And so there's now a lot of resources,

00:56:34.320 --> 00:56:35.640
not just the reporting I've done,

00:56:35.640 --> 00:56:39.120
but there's a tremendous amount
of stuff that's been written

00:56:39.120 --> 00:56:42.300
that's really easy to digest
and read and pass around.

00:56:42.300 --> 00:56:44.970
There are teachers who are
writing really great blogs.

00:56:44.970 --> 00:56:47.010
There are cognitive
scientists who are starting

00:56:47.010 --> 00:56:50.100
to really learn how to
translate this into practice.

00:56:50.100 --> 00:56:51.570
I think that's part of the problem too,

00:56:51.570 --> 00:56:53.250
it still is.

00:56:53.250 --> 00:56:55.230
Research into practice is very difficult.

00:56:55.230 --> 00:56:57.750
It takes a long time for
research to get into practice

00:56:57.750 --> 00:57:01.260
and where the science of reading
is really well established.

00:57:01.260 --> 00:57:04.170
I think it's less well
established and well tested

00:57:04.170 --> 00:57:06.690
that we have lots of
great curriculum out there

00:57:06.690 --> 00:57:07.980
that we know really work.

00:57:07.980 --> 00:57:10.590
We don't, because part of the problem is

00:57:10.590 --> 00:57:13.470
that balanced literacy
has been so predominant.

00:57:13.470 --> 00:57:15.270
It's really what the market wanted.

00:57:15.270 --> 00:57:17.430
It's what all the
publishers were creating.

00:57:17.430 --> 00:57:19.140
It's what people were buying.

00:57:19.140 --> 00:57:21.480
So there's not a huge number of options

00:57:21.480 --> 00:57:23.340
when it comes to stuff
that's better aligned

00:57:23.340 --> 00:57:24.330
with the science of reading.

00:57:24.330 --> 00:57:25.620
So I think we're in a moment now

00:57:25.620 --> 00:57:27.450
where the market's shaking out.

00:57:27.450 --> 00:57:29.400
We're gonna see a lot
of products get revised.

00:57:29.400 --> 00:57:31.380
We're gonna see new products out there

00:57:31.380 --> 00:57:32.970
and I think some of
these things will be good

00:57:32.970 --> 00:57:34.020
and they need to be tested,

00:57:34.020 --> 00:57:36.120
and a lot of them haven't been tested yet.

00:57:37.710 --> 00:57:40.357
- Great, we received a
couple of questions about,

00:57:40.357 --> 00:57:43.620
people wanted to know
how the lessons apply

00:57:43.620 --> 00:57:45.480
to particular groups of students

00:57:45.480 --> 00:57:47.610
who might have special needs.

00:57:47.610 --> 00:57:48.540
So, for example,

00:57:48.540 --> 00:57:50.550
multilingual students, or students

00:57:50.550 --> 00:57:52.680
who lack a rich vocabulary.

00:57:52.680 --> 00:57:55.650
Are there any ways in which the findings

00:57:55.650 --> 00:57:57.210
of this research suggest

00:57:57.210 --> 00:57:59.730
that teaching should be
modified for those groups,

00:57:59.730 --> 00:58:01.470
or are what you're finding that it works

00:58:01.470 --> 00:58:03.600
for all groups of students?

00:58:03.600 --> 00:58:04.433
- Well, again,

00:58:04.433 --> 00:58:05.490
it's one of the reasons why I spent time

00:58:05.490 --> 00:58:06.660
on that Simple View of Reading.

00:58:06.660 --> 00:58:08.640
'Cause I think if you even just
think about those questions

00:58:08.640 --> 00:58:10.230
and then think about the
Simple View of Reading,

00:58:10.230 --> 00:58:12.360
you can kind of find your answer, right.

00:58:12.360 --> 00:58:14.940
So there are two big
things that are involved

00:58:14.940 --> 00:58:16.380
in beginning to reading comprehension,

00:58:16.380 --> 00:58:17.400
your decoding ability,

00:58:17.400 --> 00:58:18.780
your ability to read those written words,

00:58:18.780 --> 00:58:20.310
and your language comprehension.

00:58:20.310 --> 00:58:22.530
So you clearly have
some kids, for example,

00:58:22.530 --> 00:58:24.030
a kid who doesn't speak English,

00:58:24.030 --> 00:58:25.170
who's gonna need more,

00:58:25.170 --> 00:58:27.360
a lot more on the language
comprehension side.

00:58:27.360 --> 00:58:29.640
They're just gonna need
a heavy dose of that

00:58:29.640 --> 00:58:31.890
and they are not gonna
do well in a classroom

00:58:31.890 --> 00:58:34.560
that is not providing a good base of that,

00:58:34.560 --> 00:58:36.810
and where they're even
getting more help, right.

00:58:36.810 --> 00:58:38.130
So you want lots of conversation,

00:58:38.130 --> 00:58:39.330
lots of reading out loud,

00:58:39.330 --> 00:58:40.163
lots of talking,

00:58:40.163 --> 00:58:42.330
lots of oral language,

00:58:42.330 --> 00:58:43.680
but they need the decoding too.

00:58:43.680 --> 00:58:44.670
There's no difference.

00:58:44.670 --> 00:58:47.620
They still need to learn how
to decode those written words.

00:58:49.150 --> 00:58:50.340
If you think about it,

00:58:50.340 --> 00:58:54.090
they don't have the benefit
of the oral language ability

00:58:54.090 --> 00:58:55.950
in that moment that it clicks.

00:58:55.950 --> 00:58:57.060
Like, oh yeah, I know that word.

00:58:57.060 --> 00:58:58.110
They might be learning the meaning

00:58:58.110 --> 00:58:59.700
of the word at the same time.

00:58:59.700 --> 00:59:01.860
Same thing for kids who just might not

00:59:01.860 --> 00:59:03.150
have a lot of vocabulary, right,

00:59:03.150 --> 00:59:06.900
and this is why getting that
part of it is so important.

00:59:06.900 --> 00:59:08.910
What is actually really kind of crazy

00:59:08.910 --> 00:59:10.740
about all this is teaching kids how

00:59:10.740 --> 00:59:14.250
to read the words is
arguably the easy part,

00:59:14.250 --> 00:59:16.500
and that's the part that we've
really been getting wrong

00:59:16.500 --> 00:59:18.840
and we've been arguing about for so long.

00:59:18.840 --> 00:59:23.130
That is actually arguably
easier than all the rest of it.

00:59:23.130 --> 00:59:25.020
The language comprehension, the knowledge,

00:59:25.020 --> 00:59:26.880
all that stuff that it takes to comprehend

00:59:26.880 --> 00:59:29.160
what you read requires years of schooling

00:59:29.160 --> 00:59:32.430
and is particularly
susceptible to differences

00:59:32.430 --> 00:59:33.930
in background, right.

00:59:33.930 --> 00:59:36.710
So what you know,

00:59:36.710 --> 00:59:39.510
the words you know isn't just
what you learn in school.

00:59:39.510 --> 00:59:44.010
In fact, most of what you learn
is just in your daily life,

00:59:44.010 --> 00:59:45.240
what's going on in your home,

00:59:45.240 --> 00:59:47.190
who are you getting a chance to talk to,

00:59:47.190 --> 00:59:49.080
what are the things
you're getting exposed to,

00:59:49.080 --> 00:59:50.610
all of that kind of stuff.

00:59:50.610 --> 00:59:52.950
So really equalizing things

00:59:52.950 --> 00:59:56.220
on the language comprehension
side is more difficult.

00:59:56.220 --> 00:59:58.350
We will probably always see

00:59:58.350 --> 01:00:01.410
that reading comprehension is associated

01:00:01.410 --> 01:00:04.050
with family background
and sort of opportunities

01:00:04.050 --> 01:00:05.670
for language comprehension.

01:00:05.670 --> 01:00:06.930
But I would just keep going back

01:00:06.930 --> 01:00:08.010
to the Simple View of Reading.

01:00:08.010 --> 01:00:10.080
Again, it doesn't say reading is simple,

01:00:10.080 --> 01:00:11.340
it's a Simple View,

01:00:11.340 --> 01:00:13.410
a simple way to understand
a complex process.

01:00:13.410 --> 01:00:15.450
But I think it helps disentangle

01:00:15.450 --> 01:00:17.670
what does a second-language learner,

01:00:17.670 --> 01:00:20.070
what does a kid who doesn't
have a huge vocabulary need,

01:00:20.070 --> 01:00:21.220
all that kind of stuff.

01:00:22.410 --> 01:00:24.450
- We also received a, I guess,

01:00:24.450 --> 01:00:25.980
a cognitive science question.

01:00:25.980 --> 01:00:26.880
And that is,

01:00:26.880 --> 01:00:29.520
somebody who wanted to know
whether you could compare

01:00:29.520 --> 01:00:31.710
the process that goes on in the brain

01:00:31.710 --> 01:00:33.000
with learning to read,

01:00:33.000 --> 01:00:34.920
with learning to read music?

01:00:34.920 --> 01:00:37.110
Is that similar or different?

01:00:37.110 --> 01:00:38.808
- I can't answer that question.

01:00:38.808 --> 01:00:43.620
I certainly know that learning
to read music is used often

01:00:43.620 --> 01:00:45.630
as a metaphor in terms of the question

01:00:45.630 --> 01:00:47.550
of the explicit
instruction that what needs

01:00:47.550 --> 01:00:48.813
to learn to read music.

01:00:50.370 --> 01:00:52.410
Literally how that goes on in the brain,

01:00:52.410 --> 01:00:53.820
I don't know.

01:00:53.820 --> 01:00:55.260
But I think that's fascinating.

01:00:55.260 --> 01:00:57.717
- And then, a follow-up is,

01:00:57.717 --> 01:00:58.807
"Is there a parallel

01:00:58.807 --> 01:01:01.413
"to the science of reading
to math instruction?

01:01:02.939 --> 01:01:06.000
"Do you know anything about
the state of our effort

01:01:06.000 --> 01:01:08.640
to provide evidence-based math instruction

01:01:08.640 --> 01:01:09.750
in the schools today?

01:01:09.750 --> 01:01:10.927
- Well, I showed you that book,

01:01:10.927 --> 01:01:12.450
"Reading in the Brain",

01:01:12.450 --> 01:01:14.280
and that guy, Stanislas,

01:01:14.280 --> 01:01:15.480
and I'm gonna pronounce his name wrong.

01:01:15.480 --> 01:01:17.823
He's French, but it's D-E-H-A-E-N-E.

01:01:18.870 --> 01:01:19.950
The first book he wrote,

01:01:19.950 --> 01:01:20.783
maybe it's not the first book,

01:01:20.783 --> 01:01:22.500
but before he wrote that book
about "Reading in the Brain",

01:01:22.500 --> 01:01:23.910
he wrote a book about math.

01:01:23.910 --> 01:01:25.950
I'm forgetting what it's
called at the moment.

01:01:25.950 --> 01:01:27.390
And I would say, so yes,

01:01:27.390 --> 01:01:29.400
there is a lot of research about math

01:01:29.400 --> 01:01:31.620
and how we learn math and
how that works in the brain.

01:01:31.620 --> 01:01:34.470
And I think that math
instruction suffers from some

01:01:34.470 --> 01:01:37.110
of the same problems that
reading instruction has.

01:01:37.110 --> 01:01:38.820
And it has to do with the question,

01:01:38.820 --> 01:01:42.000
with a sort of resistance
in American education,

01:01:42.000 --> 01:01:44.490
generally, to sort of explicit
and direct instruction.

01:01:44.490 --> 01:01:48.330
I would say the sort of
emphasis in instruction

01:01:48.330 --> 01:01:49.900
has more been on

01:01:52.320 --> 01:01:54.420
individual learning, learning on your own,

01:01:54.420 --> 01:01:55.890
discovering things for yourself,

01:01:55.890 --> 01:01:57.420
a more progressive approach,

01:01:57.420 --> 01:01:58.530
which I think is wonderful.

01:01:58.530 --> 01:01:59.970
That's what I wanted for my kids,

01:01:59.970 --> 01:02:01.720
when my kids were little, for sure.

01:02:03.150 --> 01:02:06.180
But I really think that it
leaves a lot of kids behind.

01:02:06.180 --> 01:02:07.920
There are things that we can explicitly

01:02:07.920 --> 01:02:09.750
and directly teach to
kids that we should be,

01:02:09.750 --> 01:02:11.910
and it doesn't have to be rote,

01:02:11.910 --> 01:02:13.710
old-fashioned, boring,

01:02:13.710 --> 01:02:15.120
abusive, any of these,

01:02:15.120 --> 01:02:17.010
whatever these ideas are that people have

01:02:17.010 --> 01:02:18.360
in their head about that.

01:02:18.360 --> 01:02:19.560
It doesn't have to be done that way.

01:02:19.560 --> 01:02:20.520
It's not being done that way.

01:02:20.520 --> 01:02:21.780
There are lots of schools where this kind

01:02:21.780 --> 01:02:23.790
of instruction is being done really well.

01:02:23.790 --> 01:02:27.570
But I think that math and reading suffer

01:02:27.570 --> 01:02:30.450
from the same idea that
somehow we don't want

01:02:30.450 --> 01:02:33.428
to explicitly teach
little children things,

01:02:33.428 --> 01:02:35.760
that we want them to
discover it on their own,

01:02:35.760 --> 01:02:37.380
that we think that that's better.

01:02:37.380 --> 01:02:41.190
And the cognitive science
research does not show

01:02:41.190 --> 01:02:42.270
that to be true,

01:02:42.270 --> 01:02:45.120
not better and not more efficient.

01:02:45.120 --> 01:02:47.010
And efficiency matters here.

01:02:47.010 --> 01:02:48.780
Efficiency really matters with reading

01:02:48.780 --> 01:02:49.950
'cause it's a multiplicative,

01:02:49.950 --> 01:02:52.050
it's an exponential thing, right.

01:02:52.050 --> 01:02:53.580
So you start to get good at reading

01:02:53.580 --> 01:02:56.130
and you get better and better
and better at it by reading.

01:02:56.130 --> 01:02:56.963
And the kids,

01:02:56.963 --> 01:02:58.110
you just spiral up.

01:02:58.110 --> 01:03:00.300
There's this thing called the
Matthew Effect in reading,

01:03:00.300 --> 01:03:01.800
right, the rich get richer,

01:03:01.800 --> 01:03:03.600
and it happens really fast.

01:03:03.600 --> 01:03:05.670
Kids who get off to a
good start in reading,

01:03:05.670 --> 01:03:08.730
like reading and then do
it more and like it more

01:03:08.730 --> 01:03:10.440
and get better at it and do it more.

01:03:10.440 --> 01:03:12.660
And most of what you know about language

01:03:12.660 --> 01:03:13.980
and the vocabulary you know

01:03:13.980 --> 01:03:16.440
and the knowledge comes from reading.

01:03:16.440 --> 01:03:17.790
So if you get to, often,

01:03:17.790 --> 01:03:19.410
not a good start with
reading, it's frustrating,

01:03:19.410 --> 01:03:20.970
it's hard and you don't like it,

01:03:20.970 --> 01:03:21.972
you resist it,

01:03:21.972 --> 01:03:24.390
you are falling so far behind.

01:03:24.390 --> 01:03:25.590
Not because you're falling behind,

01:03:25.590 --> 01:03:27.240
you're just like slightly getting,

01:03:27.240 --> 01:03:30.330
you're going like this and
your peers are going like this.

01:03:30.330 --> 01:03:33.723
This is the achievement
gap in America, right here.

01:03:35.550 --> 01:03:36.610
- Emily, I'm sorry,

01:03:36.610 --> 01:03:40.503
could I just follow-up just
quickly on the math point.

01:03:43.770 --> 01:03:44.853
I'm wondering,

01:03:45.780 --> 01:03:46.950
I don't know,

01:03:46.950 --> 01:03:48.640
is there a parallel

01:03:49.830 --> 01:03:51.550
podcast to be made

01:03:52.530 --> 01:03:54.813
about math instruction that, you know,

01:03:56.230 --> 01:03:58.260
here's what we're doing in the schools

01:03:58.260 --> 01:04:02.640
and here's a narrative
about why it happened

01:04:02.640 --> 01:04:04.353
and why it makes sense?

01:04:05.370 --> 01:04:06.203
- Yes.

01:04:07.290 --> 01:04:08.340
- Okay.
(men chuckling)

01:04:08.340 --> 01:04:10.020
- Yes, and I think
someone else should do it

01:04:10.020 --> 01:04:10.853
because I have a whole bunch

01:04:10.853 --> 01:04:12.237
of things I still wanna do about reading.

01:04:12.237 --> 01:04:13.070
- No, I hear you.
(Emily chuckling)

01:04:13.070 --> 01:04:13.980
But what I mean is,

01:04:13.980 --> 01:04:16.140
is there the same level,

01:04:16.140 --> 01:04:19.080
but not yet surfaced to this degree,

01:04:19.080 --> 01:04:23.637
the same level of scientific foundation

01:04:24.540 --> 01:04:29.540
for thinking about how
we teach basic numeracy

01:04:31.050 --> 01:04:33.524
that hasn't yet filtered--
- Yes.

01:04:33.524 --> 01:04:36.750
- Into the teacher training
and instruction, please?

01:04:36.750 --> 01:04:37.680
- Yes. I think that,

01:04:37.680 --> 01:04:41.070
yes, I think that is the case yes.

01:04:41.070 --> 01:04:41.903
- Wow.

01:04:43.650 --> 01:04:46.000
That's breaking, I'm not a journalist but

01:04:47.310 --> 01:04:49.701
that strikes me as somewhat
noteworthy, actually.

01:04:49.701 --> 01:04:52.618
(people chuckling)

01:04:53.596 --> 01:04:54.900
Yeah.

01:04:54.900 --> 01:04:57.990
- Well, I don't that there's
quite the same level.

01:04:57.990 --> 01:05:00.107
Again, I'm not an expert in that area.

01:05:00.107 --> 01:05:02.041
I know some about it.

01:05:02.041 --> 01:05:04.140
So I don't know that
there's the same level

01:05:04.140 --> 01:05:06.960
or amount of research and I don't think,

01:05:06.960 --> 01:05:08.970
well, I was just gonna say there hasn't

01:05:08.970 --> 01:05:10.560
been the equivalent of a
national reading panel,

01:05:10.560 --> 01:05:11.550
but that's actually not true.

01:05:11.550 --> 01:05:13.500
There have been some big synthesis reports

01:05:13.500 --> 01:05:15.120
about math instruction.

01:05:15.120 --> 01:05:17.460
So yes, I actually think
this is really a story

01:05:17.460 --> 01:05:20.010
that needs to be dug into.

01:05:20.010 --> 01:05:22.740
- Well, fortunately there's a number

01:05:22.740 --> 01:05:24.630
of people in the audience, maybe.

01:05:24.630 --> 01:05:25.546
- Yeah, journalism students.
- That wanna

01:05:25.546 --> 01:05:28.283
take on that challenge.
- Journalism students,

01:05:28.283 --> 01:05:29.790
soon-to-be Yale grads,

01:05:29.790 --> 01:05:32.130
go for it.
- Thanks a lot.

01:05:32.130 --> 01:05:33.870
- So there are a number
of questions, I think,

01:05:33.870 --> 01:05:35.940
of people just really trying to understand

01:05:35.940 --> 01:05:39.090
what this really means in
practice for their children.

01:05:39.090 --> 01:05:43.980
Perhaps they've already been
exposed to cueing approaches.

01:05:43.980 --> 01:05:45.667
So one question was,

01:05:45.667 --> 01:05:47.467
"Exposure to rhyming books,

01:05:47.467 --> 01:05:48.300
"is that okay?"

01:05:48.300 --> 01:05:50.137
Another one, "Is it okay

01:05:50.137 --> 01:05:53.257
"if young readers memorize
sight words in addition

01:05:53.257 --> 01:05:54.727
"to sounding out words,

01:05:54.727 --> 01:05:56.767
"is that effective as long
as they're not looking

01:05:56.767 --> 01:05:58.710
"at pictures as cues?"

01:05:58.710 --> 01:05:59.940
People are really, I think,

01:05:59.940 --> 01:06:01.297
trying to understand.

01:06:01.297 --> 01:06:03.397
"How do I know if my child,

01:06:03.397 --> 01:06:05.677
"if somebody I care about
is learning correctly

01:06:05.677 --> 01:06:08.077
"and when should I be concerned
that they're being taught

01:06:08.077 --> 01:06:11.947
"in a way that might
undermine their development

01:06:11.947 --> 01:06:12.780
"as effective readers?"

01:06:12.780 --> 01:06:14.220
- Yeah, yeah. Yeah.

01:06:14.220 --> 01:06:15.053
Yeah, I mean,

01:06:15.053 --> 01:06:16.680
it's true that I think when
you point out these things,

01:06:16.680 --> 01:06:19.680
it's makes a lot of sense
that people get very anxious

01:06:19.680 --> 01:06:20.550
and nervous about it.

01:06:20.550 --> 01:06:21.383
I will say one thing,

01:06:21.383 --> 01:06:23.607
so lots of kids are very resilient, right.

01:06:23.607 --> 01:06:25.020
And lots of kids, really,

01:06:25.020 --> 01:06:27.180
they can be taught this cueing stuff

01:06:27.180 --> 01:06:30.240
and they will figure out
that the sounding out part,

01:06:30.240 --> 01:06:31.410
that one cue,

01:06:31.410 --> 01:06:32.760
that's the best, most effective,

01:06:32.760 --> 01:06:35.550
most efficient, most accurate
way to know the words,

01:06:35.550 --> 01:06:37.742
and they get that and
they start doing that.

01:06:37.742 --> 01:06:41.460
I have to say that I've
talked to a lot of tutors,

01:06:41.460 --> 01:06:42.777
reading tutors, who
work with middle school

01:06:42.777 --> 01:06:43.890
and high school kids.

01:06:43.890 --> 01:06:46.560
Sometimes this stuff really reveals itself

01:06:46.560 --> 01:06:49.380
when kids are first taking
the ACT and the SAT,

01:06:49.380 --> 01:06:52.126
because it's like a huge
amount of reading really fast.

01:06:52.126 --> 01:06:54.510
That's when some of those
reading problems really

01:06:54.510 --> 01:06:56.850
get revealed for the
first time to parents.

01:06:56.850 --> 01:06:59.490
And they take them to
tutoring and the tutors say

01:06:59.490 --> 01:07:02.670
that breaking these
habits are really hard,

01:07:02.670 --> 01:07:05.580
and they have to really work
on breaking the habit first.

01:07:05.580 --> 01:07:07.140
And this cueing stuff is stuff

01:07:07.140 --> 01:07:08.970
that you don't even
necessarily need to be taught.

01:07:08.970 --> 01:07:12.120
It's what a lot of readers
will come up with on their own

01:07:12.120 --> 01:07:15.150
in the absence of being
taught how to read the words

01:07:15.150 --> 01:07:16.920
and they can't figure it out.

01:07:16.920 --> 01:07:17.940
That said, you know,

01:07:17.940 --> 01:07:19.173
kids are resilient.

01:07:21.120 --> 01:07:24.180
And people, human beings
are very responsive

01:07:24.180 --> 01:07:25.013
to good instruction.

01:07:25.013 --> 01:07:27.570
So I think good instruction really can

01:07:27.570 --> 01:07:29.640
and does work and works at all ages.

01:07:29.640 --> 01:07:31.470
I've talked to a number of adults

01:07:31.470 --> 01:07:34.530
who really were subliterate
into their forties

01:07:34.530 --> 01:07:36.570
and fifties and got some good instruction

01:07:36.570 --> 01:07:38.640
and really were able to read.

01:07:38.640 --> 01:07:40.277
Rhyming books are fantastic.

01:07:40.277 --> 01:07:43.053
There's all kinds of things
that if you think about.

01:07:44.460 --> 01:07:46.620
Rhyming books are really about language

01:07:46.620 --> 01:07:48.900
and the facility with language
and understanding words

01:07:48.900 --> 01:07:51.390
and those are really good and fine.

01:07:51.390 --> 01:07:54.060
Do you want that to be what you're using

01:07:54.060 --> 01:07:56.550
when you're trying to teach
kids how to decode words?

01:07:56.550 --> 01:07:59.430
No, because it's gonna be too
easy for them to get them,

01:07:59.430 --> 01:08:01.050
based on the rhyme, right.

01:08:01.050 --> 01:08:01.883
So it depends.

01:08:01.883 --> 01:08:03.153
It's like for what purpose?

01:08:04.132 --> 01:08:06.420
There are certain books
and certain activities

01:08:06.420 --> 01:08:07.740
for certain purposes.

01:08:07.740 --> 01:08:10.140
On the question of memorizing words.

01:08:10.140 --> 01:08:11.190
So, no.

01:08:11.190 --> 01:08:13.560
I think that one of the
things we really do know is

01:08:13.560 --> 01:08:15.300
that whole idea of just flashcards,

01:08:15.300 --> 01:08:16.680
memorize the words,

01:08:16.680 --> 01:08:18.930
it's not the most
efficient and effective way

01:08:18.930 --> 01:08:20.070
to learn the words.

01:08:20.070 --> 01:08:21.870
Is it gonna be damaging to all kids?

01:08:21.870 --> 01:08:24.060
Absolutely not, because
some of those kids really

01:08:24.060 --> 01:08:26.490
are teaching themselves
the code through that.

01:08:26.490 --> 01:08:27.570
They're memorizing the words.

01:08:27.570 --> 01:08:28.830
They're putting together the spelling,

01:08:28.830 --> 01:08:31.350
the sound of the meaning
and it's making sense.

01:08:31.350 --> 01:08:36.330
But that is not a good sign if
what your kid is being asked

01:08:36.330 --> 01:08:39.300
to do in school is just
memorize a lot of words,

01:08:39.300 --> 01:08:40.920
because that is actually not the best,

01:08:40.920 --> 01:08:44.910
most efficient way to store
them in your long-term memory.

01:08:44.910 --> 01:08:48.180
Now again, some kids really
are teaching themselves

01:08:48.180 --> 01:08:50.130
and they can use these pattern books

01:08:50.130 --> 01:08:51.960
and they can be taught
the cueing strategies

01:08:51.960 --> 01:08:53.220
and all of that exposure,

01:08:53.220 --> 01:08:54.840
they put it together and they're good.

01:08:54.840 --> 01:08:55.860
It's almost like magic,

01:08:55.860 --> 01:08:57.363
like my kids.

01:08:59.340 --> 01:09:02.340
- So, I'll ask a question
myself if that's okay.

01:09:02.340 --> 01:09:05.970
So thanks to your amazing
podcast and parental advocacy,

01:09:05.970 --> 01:09:08.280
we are seeing more and
more states that are trying

01:09:08.280 --> 01:09:10.890
to promote science of reading

01:09:10.890 --> 01:09:13.950
and to eliminate cueing
strategies and to be effective.

01:09:13.950 --> 01:09:15.690
But, of course at the end of the day,

01:09:15.690 --> 01:09:18.480
this still actually has to
happen in the classroom.

01:09:18.480 --> 01:09:22.410
Teachers have to change in
some cases the way they've

01:09:22.410 --> 01:09:23.820
been teaching for many years.

01:09:23.820 --> 01:09:25.380
How difficult is that?

01:09:25.380 --> 01:09:27.570
How much professional
development is required?

01:09:27.570 --> 01:09:29.490
How many hours of training?

01:09:29.490 --> 01:09:31.200
What are the challenges to actually,

01:09:31.200 --> 01:09:33.150
if a state has already made a commitment?

01:09:33.150 --> 01:09:34.710
They've listened to your podcast,

01:09:34.710 --> 01:09:36.030
the parents came to the school board,

01:09:36.030 --> 01:09:38.190
they said, we absolutely wanna do this,

01:09:38.190 --> 01:09:39.630
how hard will it be to go

01:09:39.630 --> 01:09:42.390
from that commitment at a policy level,

01:09:42.390 --> 01:09:44.430
to actually making sure this happens

01:09:44.430 --> 01:09:47.790
in classrooms consistently
over the long run?

01:09:47.790 --> 01:09:49.110
- Hard?
(Emily chuckling)

01:09:49.110 --> 01:09:50.520
I think it's gonna be very hard

01:09:50.520 --> 01:09:52.020
and I really worry about that.

01:09:52.890 --> 01:09:54.180
I mean, yeah.

01:09:54.180 --> 01:09:56.730
It's gonna be very hard
'cause this is a really big

01:09:57.778 --> 01:09:59.430
amount for teachers to learn.

01:09:59.430 --> 01:10:00.420
There are a lot of things that they need

01:10:00.420 --> 01:10:02.610
to unlearn along the way.

01:10:02.610 --> 01:10:05.700
I'll just sort of report on
what teachers have told me.

01:10:05.700 --> 01:10:07.473
I hear from teachers all the time.

01:10:09.305 --> 01:10:12.240
It's almost like these
stages of grief about this.

01:10:12.240 --> 01:10:14.070
Often, they resist at first,

01:10:14.070 --> 01:10:14.903
like, oh no, no, no,

01:10:14.903 --> 01:10:15.736
that can't be.

01:10:15.736 --> 01:10:17.010
I've been teaching kids this way,

01:10:17.010 --> 01:10:18.750
and a lot of them are doing fine,

01:10:18.750 --> 01:10:20.070
and a lot of them were doing fine, right,

01:10:20.070 --> 01:10:21.710
'cause a lot of kids do fine,

01:10:21.710 --> 01:10:23.460
depending on where you are, right.

01:10:23.460 --> 01:10:25.140
You might have most of the
kids in your class are fine,

01:10:25.140 --> 01:10:26.010
no matter how you teach,

01:10:26.010 --> 01:10:29.100
and in other places you
might have a lot more kids.

01:10:29.100 --> 01:10:29.977
So many teachers though have been like,

01:10:29.977 --> 01:10:32.100
you know, I had a gut level feeling

01:10:32.100 --> 01:10:33.780
that something wasn't right.

01:10:33.780 --> 01:10:35.580
And a lot of times they blame themselves.

01:10:35.580 --> 01:10:36.780
I wasn't doing it well enough.

01:10:36.780 --> 01:10:37.950
I wasn't doing good enough job,

01:10:37.950 --> 01:10:39.330
so I'm gonna work really hard,

01:10:39.330 --> 01:10:41.100
I'm gonna go to more
professional development,

01:10:41.100 --> 01:10:42.420
I'm really gonna do this.

01:10:42.420 --> 01:10:43.253
They work hard,

01:10:43.253 --> 01:10:44.247
do everything they're supposed to do

01:10:44.247 --> 01:10:45.900
and a lot of the kids
still aren't learning.

01:10:45.900 --> 01:10:46.733
So what do you do?

01:10:46.733 --> 01:10:48.119
Oh well, it must be the kids.

01:10:48.119 --> 01:10:49.530
They're not working hard enough.

01:10:49.530 --> 01:10:51.120
They're lazy, they're
not trying hard enough.

01:10:51.120 --> 01:10:52.860
Oh, maybe he has a reading disability.

01:10:52.860 --> 01:10:55.500
Oh, her mother didn't read
to her enough at home.

01:10:55.500 --> 01:10:58.200
Poor girl, she doesn't have
enough books in her home.

01:10:58.200 --> 01:11:01.230
And so when teachers
start to learn about this,

01:11:01.230 --> 01:11:02.070
they start to realize,

01:11:02.070 --> 01:11:02.903
oh my god,

01:11:02.903 --> 01:11:03.870
I was blaming myself,

01:11:03.870 --> 01:11:05.160
I was blaming the families,

01:11:05.160 --> 01:11:06.510
I was blaming the kids,

01:11:06.510 --> 01:11:07.980
but there was a lot of
stuff I didn't know.

01:11:07.980 --> 01:11:10.530
And now I have a huge
amount I need to know.

01:11:10.530 --> 01:11:13.140
And so, a lot of teachers
are making investments

01:11:13.140 --> 01:11:14.490
of a huge amount of their time,

01:11:14.490 --> 01:11:16.230
and in some cases their money,

01:11:16.230 --> 01:11:17.250
to go learn this stuff.

01:11:17.250 --> 01:11:19.440
And teaching is a hard job.

01:11:19.440 --> 01:11:21.330
We ask so much of our teachers.

01:11:21.330 --> 01:11:22.710
The work conditions aren't great.

01:11:22.710 --> 01:11:23.905
We don't pay them enough.

01:11:23.905 --> 01:11:25.230
This is not easy.

01:11:25.230 --> 01:11:27.450
There are plenty of teachers
who are resisting this

01:11:27.450 --> 01:11:28.770
for good reason.

01:11:28.770 --> 01:11:30.840
And one of the reasons
they're resisting it is, like,

01:11:30.840 --> 01:11:32.820
I have heard you telling me before

01:11:32.820 --> 01:11:33.957
that this is the new thing I have to learn

01:11:33.957 --> 01:11:37.170
and the new thing I have to
do and I am not interested.

01:11:37.170 --> 01:11:40.290
I'm gonna close my door and do
what I've always been doing.

01:11:40.290 --> 01:11:41.490
And in some cases,

01:11:41.490 --> 01:11:43.500
what they've always been doing is great.

01:11:43.500 --> 01:11:46.924
They figured this out
long ago and were teaching

01:11:46.924 --> 01:11:49.680
and they were trying
to resist all the fads

01:11:49.680 --> 01:11:51.180
and all the things going on.

01:11:51.180 --> 01:11:54.330
Unfortunately, we'd love to
be able to tell teachers,

01:11:54.330 --> 01:11:55.163
close your doors

01:11:55.163 --> 01:11:56.820
and just make your own
professional decisions.

01:11:56.820 --> 01:11:59.820
But so many teachers haven't
learned what they need to know

01:11:59.820 --> 01:12:02.070
about how kids learn to read
and they're recognizing that.

01:12:02.070 --> 01:12:03.990
So I think this is a really,

01:12:03.990 --> 01:12:05.070
really big challenge.

01:12:05.070 --> 01:12:06.300
I guess I will just say,

01:12:06.300 --> 01:12:08.520
I'm hopeful at the moment
because I think there are a lot

01:12:08.520 --> 01:12:10.397
of people out there who
are really committed to it,

01:12:10.397 --> 01:12:13.083
'cause it's so important.

01:12:14.351 --> 01:12:16.830
- Emily, I just wanna say one

01:12:16.830 --> 01:12:19.113
of the participants recommended,

01:12:21.210 --> 01:12:22.043
I guess,

01:12:23.388 --> 01:12:24.690
I don't know what it is.

01:12:24.690 --> 01:12:26.313
It's a website, I guess.

01:12:29.460 --> 01:12:30.477
Chalk &amp; Talk,

01:12:30.477 --> 01:12:32.400
with an ampersand.

01:12:32.400 --> 01:12:33.270
- Yeah.

01:12:33.270 --> 01:12:36.534
- Anna Stokke on mathematics education.

01:12:36.534 --> 01:12:39.043
- On math, okay.
- Yeah.

01:12:39.927 --> 01:12:41.220
And I just wanted to know,

01:12:41.220 --> 01:12:44.850
are there any sources that you turn to

01:12:44.850 --> 01:12:47.100
or that you find especially interesting,

01:12:47.100 --> 01:12:51.720
any internet websites that you would say

01:12:51.720 --> 01:12:55.560
if someone wants to know
more about this other,

01:12:55.560 --> 01:12:58.110
that you would recommend they turn to that

01:12:58.110 --> 01:13:00.210
additional information?
- Sure, if you go

01:13:00.210 --> 01:13:02.340
to the "Sold a Story" webpage,

01:13:02.340 --> 01:13:03.780
which is soldastory.org,

01:13:03.780 --> 01:13:06.390
there's a reading list there
and I just put 10 things on it.

01:13:06.390 --> 01:13:08.070
I literally could've put a hundred.

01:13:08.070 --> 01:13:09.840
But if you start with those 10 things,

01:13:09.840 --> 01:13:11.700
you can go down a rabbit hole for the rest

01:13:11.700 --> 01:13:12.720
of your life if you want to,

01:13:12.720 --> 01:13:14.190
if you just follow the footnotes.

01:13:14.190 --> 01:13:17.550
There's one paper on there
that has like 250 references

01:13:17.550 --> 01:13:20.910
or something so you'll be good.

01:13:20.910 --> 01:13:22.620
- Fantastic.

01:13:22.620 --> 01:13:25.380
And we're coming to the end of our time,

01:13:25.380 --> 01:13:28.020
so are there any sort of observations

01:13:28.020 --> 01:13:32.220
or anything you'd like to say kind of

01:13:32.220 --> 01:13:34.533
to round out our discussion?

01:13:35.970 --> 01:13:37.860
- Wow, okay.

01:13:37.860 --> 01:13:39.690
Well, thanks for being interested in this.

01:13:39.690 --> 01:13:41.520
I know people are
interested in it for a bunch

01:13:41.520 --> 01:13:43.530
of different reasons and so I appreciate

01:13:43.530 --> 01:13:45.060
whatever reason you bring to the table.

01:13:45.060 --> 01:13:46.680
Whether it's your own child,

01:13:46.680 --> 01:13:49.800
whether you're a teacher and
are trying to learn better,

01:13:49.800 --> 01:13:52.020
whether you're a student who
wants to become a teacher

01:13:52.020 --> 01:13:55.560
or go into education policy
or go into education research,

01:13:55.560 --> 01:13:56.400
I do think this is sort

01:13:56.400 --> 01:13:58.260
of an all-hands-on-deck kind of situation,

01:13:58.260 --> 01:14:01.306
and I promise you that this
stuff is really fascinating.

01:14:01.306 --> 01:14:04.230
Just at an intellectual level, for me,

01:14:04.230 --> 01:14:06.300
doing this work over the
past five or six years

01:14:06.300 --> 01:14:07.470
has been so gratifying

01:14:07.470 --> 01:14:10.601
'cause it's just really interesting stuff.

01:14:10.601 --> 01:14:14.910
So I encourage you to go to
that reading list and dig in.

01:14:14.910 --> 01:14:17.010
It's just a fascinating topic.

01:14:17.010 --> 01:14:18.570
